,\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The network separates alanine formation from chirality generation. Ten experimentally supported prebiotic routes converge on an alanine pool: two aldehyde/cyanide routes, one Bucherer-Bergs route, five pyruvate-to-alanine amination variants on Fe/Ni minerals or redox surfaces, one oxime-reduction route, one transamination route, and one thiol/formose route. Pyruvate is treated as a shared proximal C3 feedstock hub because the report identifies pyruvate supply, rather than pyruvate amination itself, as the main upstream bottleneck. Because all supported synthetic routes yield racemic alanine, the final conversion to L-alanine is modeled as an explicit two-step chiral module: circularly polarized UV photolysis seeds only a small L excess, and wet-dry crystallization then amplifies it in a deliberately speculative but chemically motivated final step. Lactate is included as the main competing sink where reduction outcompetes amination.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen gas\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7NO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminobutanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aspartic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H4O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxobutanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"oxaloacetate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"rac-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-enriched alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Classical Strecker synthesis of alanine from acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Aqueous post-discharge condensate or evaporitic pool; this node compresses imine formation, cyanide addition, and hydrolysis during mild heating or wet-dry cycling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetaldehyde condenses with ammonia to an imine, cyanide adds to give an aminonitrile, and hydrolysis yields alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the canonical prebiotic alanine route. Alanine was produced in Miller-type discharge experiments and is widely interpreted as arising from Strecker chemistry once HCN and aldehydes are available (Miller, 1953). Buffered neutral-atmosphere reassessments still recover alanine, showing the route does not require an extremely reducing atmosphere, although atmospheric plausibility and local HCN availability remain contested (Cleaves et al., 2008). The product is racemic, so a separate chirality step is still required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic Strecker synthesis of alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"HS-\", \"Cu(I)\", \"phosphate\", \"UV light\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254 nm UV in sulfidic, phosphate-containing water; this node begins at acetaldehyde as the proximal aldehyde and does not expand its upstream HCN homologation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HS-/Cu photoredox maintains reduced cyanide-derived carbon chemistry, enabling acetaldehyde to undergo Strecker conversion and hydrolysis to alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. showed that cyanosulfidic chemistry can access amino-acid precursors and deliver alanine through Strecker-type steps within a broader UV-driven network (Patel et al., 2015). This route is chemically distinct from the classical Strecker setting because the same surface photoredox system also furnishes related sugars, lipids, and nucleotide precursors, but it depends on locally high cyanide and sulfide concentrations, which are a major geochemical constraint (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs conversion of pyruvate to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"CO2 or bicarbonate is required; hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates are implicit and not expanded as separate nodes.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to pyruvate, CO2 enables carbamoylation and hydantoin formation, and subsequent hydrolysis gives alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pulletikurti et al. experimentally demonstrated alpha-amino acid formation from alpha-keto acids through Bucherer-Bergs chemistry and showed that CO2 is mechanistically essential (Pulletikurti et al., 2022). This pathway is especially important because it bridges cyanide chemistry with the metabolic carbon skeleton of pyruvate, but it still inherits both the cyanide co-localization problem and the unresolved source of pyruvate itself.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"FeS and ferrous hydroxide reductive amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"freshly precipitated FeS\", \"Fe(OH)2\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-348K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate to high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS / Fe(OH)2\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline pH about 9-10; typically 0.375-1.5 M NH3; cool off-axis hydrothermal interfaces are favored over hotter vent fluids.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvate forms an imine or iminopyruvate with ammonia, and mineral-mediated electron transfer reduces it to alanine; parallel reduction gives lactate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Huber and Wachtershauser reported efficient reductive amination of alpha-keto acids with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2 under aqueous alkaline conditions, and later summaries cite alanine yields up to about 50 percent (Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003; Milner-White and Russell, 2008). Kaur et al. emphasized that these Fe-based systems generally require high ammonia and that the minerals likely function as stoichiometric reductants rather than true catalysts, so lactate is included as the major competing sink (Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Green-rust reductive amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"green rust (mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-343K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate to high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"green rust\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Best near pH 10 with about 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III); lower pH shifts selectivity toward lactate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mixed-valence iron hydroxide reduces an iminopyruvate intermediate; mineral redox state controls the alanine versus lactate outcome.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Barge et al. showed that pyruvate plus ammonia yields alanine only when the iron hydroxide is partially oxidized, with the best selectivity near pH 10 and roughly equal Fe(II) and Fe(III) (Barge et al., 2019). This is one of the strongest alkaline-vent-compatible demonstrations of alanine synthesis, but the exact mechanism remains unresolved and the chemistry is best interpreted for cool hydrothermal interfaces rather than hot vent-fluid exposure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Electro-reduced FeS amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"electro-reduced FeS (FeS_PERM)\", \"native Fe0 patches\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-298K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS_PERM\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH about 9.6; about 0.5 M NH3; vent-like negative potential near -0.7 V versus SHE is required to generate the reactive surface.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A reduced FeS surface bearing Fe0-like sites efficiently reduces an imine or iminopyruvate intermediate to alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kitadai et al. showed that electro-reduced FeS generated under vent-like potentials converts pyruvate and ammonia to alanine almost completely within 48 hours at alkaline pH (Kitadai et al., 2019). This makes the pyruvate-to-alanine node exceptionally robust, but the route depends on sustained negative potentials and on preformed reduced FeS surfaces, so its natural prevalence remains an open question.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Ni0 and H2 reductive amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"native Ni0\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-353K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni0\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 7-8 at cooler runs or more alkaline in hotter nickel-only runs; useful conversion is possible even near 6 mM NH3.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Adsorption on nickel allows imine formation from pyruvate and ammonia followed by hydrogenation with H2; competing reduction gives lactate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Native Ni or Ni-rich meteoritic metal catalyzes hydrogenative amination of pyruvate, giving substantial alanine while relaxing the high-ammonia objection raised against Fe-mineral systems (Kaur et al., 2024). The main geochemical critiques are the availability of native nickel and the need for elevated H2 pressure, and lactate remains a significant side product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxylamine and Fe0 oxime reduction of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe0\", \"Fe2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm to moderate\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe0 / Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mild iron-rich protometabolic conditions; nitrogen is supplied through hydroxylamine rather than free ammonia.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvate first forms an oxime with hydroxylamine, and Fe0 then reduces the C=N-OH functionality to the amine, yielding alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. generated alanine from pyruvate through an oxime-reduction route using hydroxylamine and Fe0 in an iron-promoted protometabolic network (Muchowska et al., 2019). The chemistry is plausible and biosynthetically congruent, but hydroxylamine availability remains the major unresolved prebiotic weakness of this pathway (Muchowska et al., 2019; Kitadai et al., 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyridoxal-metal transamination to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"pyridoxal\", \"Fe(III)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-353K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"pyridoxal / Fe(III)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Amino-group shuttling proceeds through pyridoxamine; Fe(III) strongly enhances the uncatalyzed process.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyridoxal forms a Schiff base with the donor amino acid, becomes pyridoxamine after amino-group transfer, and then transfers the amino group to pyruvate to form alanine and oxaloacetate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nonenzymatic metal-pyridoxal chemistry can shuttle amino groups to pyruvate. Dherbassy et al. showed strong Fe(III)/pyridoxal cooperativity and convergence toward the biological transamination mechanism (Dherbassy et al., 2023). This route is included as a biochemical-style alternative, but the prebiotic origin of pyridoxal is unresolved and metal effects can be system-dependent or even inhibitory in some systems (Dherbassy et al., 2023; Zabinski and Toney, 2001).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiol-catalyzed formose route to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"thiol, e.g. 3-mercaptopropionic acid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"thiol\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH about 5.2; weeks-long reaction in wet-dry shoreline pools; essentially no alanine is expected without the thiol promoter.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formose-derived carbonyls undergo dehydration to methylglyoxal-like species, thiol trapping to hemithioacetals, imine chemistry with ammonia, and hydrolysis to alanine; lactate forms in parallel.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Weber showed that thiol-catalyzed reactions of glyceraldehyde or of formaldehyde plus glycolaldehyde with ammonia generate alanine under mild aqueous conditions, with alanine appearing along with lactate and related products (Weber, 1985; Weber, 1998). This route avoids cyanide and connects sugar chemistry to amino-acid synthesis, but yields are low and the required thiol catalyst has its own prebiotic supply problem.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"CPL seed enrichment of the alanine pool\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"circularly polarized UV light\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Solid film, dry residue, or ice-like surface layer; only percent-level enantiomeric excess is expected; helicity and wavelength must favor the L enantiomer.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Differential absorption and photodestruction of the two enantiomers biases the surviving alanine pool toward a small L excess.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Circularly polarized UV light can induce small alanine enantiomeric excesses by differential photolysis or related photochemistry in solid-state and ice-analog experiments (Meinert et al., 2014; Modica et al., 2014). The effect is only percent-level and its direct terrestrial implementation is uncertain, so this step is treated only as a seed-enrichment module rather than a complete solution to the L problem.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Wet-dry amplification of a small L excess to L-alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Repeated dissolution and recrystallization in evaporitic pools or tidal flats; requires a preexisting seed excess from rxn_011.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Repeated phase partitioning and selective crystallization amplify a preexisting slight L bias until an L-dominated alanine fraction is obtained.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative amplification module. No provided study directly demonstrates alanine-specific evaporative amplification to high L excess, but such a second-stage selection step is required because all experimentally supported synthesis routes are racemic and returned extraterrestrial samples also show alanine to be racemic (Glavin et al., 2021; Glavin et al., 2025). Wet-dry crystallization is included here as a chemically plausible, but unverified, way to amplify the small L excess from rxn_011.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Classical Strecker to L-alanine\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde, HCN, and ammonia form rac-alanine by classical Strecker chemistry; CPL then seeds a small L excess that is amplified during wet-dry crystallization.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic Strecker to L-alanine\",\n      \"description\": \"A UV, HS-, Cu(I) surface network drives Strecker-type alanine formation from acetaldehyde, HCN, and ammonia, followed by the shared chiral-enrichment module.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs pyruvate route to L-alanine\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate, cyanide, ammonia, and CO2 generate alanine through Bucherer-Bergs chemistry, after which CPL and crystallization convert the racemate toward L-alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"FeS reductive amination route to L-alanine\",\n      \"description\": \"Freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2 reductively aminates pyruvate in alkaline hydrothermal settings, producing alanine with lactate competition before the shared chiral module.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Green-rust route to L-alanine\",\n      \"description\": \"Mixed-valence green rust converts pyruvate and ammonia to alanine under alkaline hydrothermal conditions, and the resulting alanine pool then enters CPL seeding and crystallization amplification.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Electro-reduced FeS vent route to L-alanine\",\n      \"description\": \"Reduced FeS surfaces generated under vent-like electrochemical potentials aminatively reduce pyruvate to alanine, then the common two-step chiral module yields L-alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Ni0 and H2 route to L-alanine\",\n      \"description\": \"Native nickel catalyzes hydrogenative amination of pyruvate with H2, producing alanine and some lactate before entry into the CPL-plus-crystallization module.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydroxylamine oxime-reduction route to L-alanine\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate reacts with hydroxylamine and is reduced by Fe0 through an oxime intermediate to alanine, which is then L-biased and amplified by the shared surface chiral module.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyridoxal transamination route to L-alanine\",\n      \"description\": \"Aspartate transfers its amino group to pyruvate through Fe(III)-assisted pyridoxal chemistry, giving alanine and oxaloacetate before the common chiral-enrichment steps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiol-formose route to L-alanine\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde react with ammonia in the presence of a thiol to produce alanine under mild surface conditions, after which the alanine pool is pushed toward L by CPL seeding and crystallization.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_012\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "The network separates alanine formation from chirality generation. Ten experimentally supported prebiotic routes converge on an alanine pool: two aldehyde/cyanide routes, one Bucherer-Bergs route, five pyruvate-to-alanine amination variants on Fe/Ni minerals or redox surfaces, one oxime-reduction route, one transamination route, and one thiol/formose route. Pyruvate is treated as a shared proximal C3 feedstock hub because the report identifies pyruvate supply, rather than pyruvate amination itself, as the main upstream bottleneck. Because all supported synthetic routes yield racemic alanine, the final conversion to L-alanine is modeled as an explicit two-step chiral module: circularly polarized UV photolysis seeds only a small L excess, and wet-dry crystallization then amplifies it in a deliberately speculative but chemically motivated final step. Lactate is included as the main competing sink where reduction outcompetes amination.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen gas", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "NH3O", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C4H7NO4", "iupac_name": "2-aminobutanedioic acid", "common_name": "aspartic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C4H4O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxobutanedioic acid", "common_name": "oxaloacetate", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "glycolaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "rac-alanine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-enriched alanine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(2S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanoic acid", "common_name": "lactate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2,4H,1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Classical Strecker synthesis of alanine from acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Aqueous post-discharge condensate or evaporitic pool; this node compresses imine formation, cyanide addition, and hydrolysis during mild heating or wet-dry cycling."}, "mechanism": "Acetaldehyde condenses with ammonia to an imine, cyanide adds to give an aminonitrile, and hydrolysis yields alanine.", "reasoning": "This is the canonical prebiotic alanine route. Alanine was produced in Miller-type discharge experiments and is widely interpreted as arising from Strecker chemistry once HCN and aldehydes are available (Miller, 1953). Buffered neutral-atmosphere reassessments still recover alanine, showing the route does not require an extremely reducing atmosphere, although atmospheric plausibility and local HCN availability remain contested (Cleaves et al., 2008). The product is racemic, so a separate chirality step is still required."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanosulfidic Strecker synthesis of alanine", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["HS-", "Cu(I)", "phosphate", "UV light"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)", "aqueous": true, "other": "254 nm UV in sulfidic, phosphate-containing water; this node begins at acetaldehyde as the proximal aldehyde and does not expand its upstream HCN homologation."}, "mechanism": "HS-/Cu photoredox maintains reduced cyanide-derived carbon chemistry, enabling acetaldehyde to undergo Strecker conversion and hydrolysis to alanine.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. showed that cyanosulfidic chemistry can access amino-acid precursors and deliver alanine through Strecker-type steps within a broader UV-driven network (Patel et al., 2015). This route is chemically distinct from the classical Strecker setting because the same surface photoredox system also furnishes related sugars, lipids, and nucleotide precursors, but it depends on locally high cyanide and sulfide concentrations, which are a major geochemical constraint (Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs conversion of pyruvate to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "298-323K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "CO2 or bicarbonate is required; hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates are implicit and not expanded as separate nodes."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to pyruvate, CO2 enables carbamoylation and hydantoin formation, and subsequent hydrolysis gives alanine.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti et al. experimentally demonstrated alpha-amino acid formation from alpha-keto acids through Bucherer-Bergs chemistry and showed that CO2 is mechanistically essential (Pulletikurti et al., 2022). This pathway is especially important because it bridges cyanide chemistry with the metabolic carbon skeleton of pyruvate, but it still inherits both the cyanide co-localization problem and the unresolved source of pyruvate itself."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "FeS and ferrous hydroxide reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_015"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["freshly precipitated FeS", "Fe(OH)2"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-348K", "pressure": "moderate to high", "catalyst": "FeS / Fe(OH)2", "aqueous": true, "other": "Alkaline pH about 9-10; typically 0.375-1.5 M NH3; cool off-axis hydrothermal interfaces are favored over hotter vent fluids."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate forms an imine or iminopyruvate with ammonia, and mineral-mediated electron transfer reduces it to alanine; parallel reduction gives lactate.", "reasoning": "Huber and Wachtershauser reported efficient reductive amination of alpha-keto acids with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2 under aqueous alkaline conditions, and later summaries cite alanine yields up to about 50 percent (Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003; Milner-White and Russell, 2008). Kaur et al. emphasized that these Fe-based systems generally require high ammonia and that the minerals likely function as stoichiometric reductants rather than true catalysts, so lactate is included as the major competing sink (Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Green-rust reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_015"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["green rust (mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-343K", "pressure": "moderate to high", "catalyst": "green rust", "aqueous": true, "other": "Best near pH 10 with about 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III); lower pH shifts selectivity toward lactate."}, "mechanism": "Mixed-valence iron hydroxide reduces an iminopyruvate intermediate; mineral redox state controls the alanine versus lactate outcome.", "reasoning": "Barge et al. showed that pyruvate plus ammonia yields alanine only when the iron hydroxide is partially oxidized, with the best selectivity near pH 10 and roughly equal Fe(II) and Fe(III) (Barge et al., 2019). This is one of the strongest alkaline-vent-compatible demonstrations of alanine synthesis, but the exact mechanism remains unresolved and the chemistry is best interpreted for cool hydrothermal interfaces rather than hot vent-fluid exposure."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Electro-reduced FeS amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["electro-reduced FeS (FeS_PERM)", "native Fe0 patches"], "conditions": {"temperature": "296-298K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "FeS_PERM", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH about 9.6; about 0.5 M NH3; vent-like negative potential near -0.7 V versus SHE is required to generate the reactive surface."}, "mechanism": "A reduced FeS surface bearing Fe0-like sites efficiently reduces an imine or iminopyruvate intermediate to alanine.", "reasoning": "Kitadai et al. showed that electro-reduced FeS generated under vent-like potentials converts pyruvate and ammonia to alanine almost completely within 48 hours at alkaline pH (Kitadai et al., 2019). This makes the pyruvate-to-alanine node exceptionally robust, but the route depends on sustained negative potentials and on preformed reduced FeS surfaces, so its natural prevalence remains an open question."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Ni0 and H2 reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_015"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["native Ni0"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-353K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni0", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 7-8 at cooler runs or more alkaline in hotter nickel-only runs; useful conversion is possible even near 6 mM NH3."}, "mechanism": "Adsorption on nickel allows imine formation from pyruvate and ammonia followed by hydrogenation with H2; competing reduction gives lactate.", "reasoning": "Native Ni or Ni-rich meteoritic metal catalyzes hydrogenative amination of pyruvate, giving substantial alanine while relaxing the high-ammonia objection raised against Fe-mineral systems (Kaur et al., 2024). The main geochemical critiques are the availability of native nickel and the need for elevated H2 pressure, and lactate remains a significant side product."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydroxylamine and Fe0 oxime reduction of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Fe0", "Fe2+"], "conditions": {"temperature": "298-323K", "pressure": "1 atm to moderate", "catalyst": "Fe0 / Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Mild iron-rich protometabolic conditions; nitrogen is supplied through hydroxylamine rather than free ammonia."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate first forms an oxime with hydroxylamine, and Fe0 then reduces the C=N-OH functionality to the amine, yielding alanine.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. generated alanine from pyruvate through an oxime-reduction route using hydroxylamine and Fe0 in an iron-promoted protometabolic network (Muchowska et al., 2019). The chemistry is plausible and biosynthetically congruent, but hydroxylamine availability remains the major unresolved prebiotic weakness of this pathway (Muchowska et al., 2019; Kitadai et al., 2019)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Pyridoxal-metal transamination to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_012"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["pyridoxal", "Fe(III)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "298-353K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "pyridoxal / Fe(III)", "aqueous": true, "other": "Amino-group shuttling proceeds through pyridoxamine; Fe(III) strongly enhances the uncatalyzed process."}, "mechanism": "Pyridoxal forms a Schiff base with the donor amino acid, becomes pyridoxamine after amino-group transfer, and then transfers the amino group to pyruvate to form alanine and oxaloacetate.", "reasoning": "Nonenzymatic metal-pyridoxal chemistry can shuttle amino groups to pyruvate. Dherbassy et al. showed strong Fe(III)/pyridoxal cooperativity and convergence toward the biological transamination mechanism (Dherbassy et al., 2023). This route is included as a biochemical-style alternative, but the prebiotic origin of pyridoxal is unresolved and metal effects can be system-dependent or even inhibitory in some systems (Dherbassy et al., 2023; Zabinski and Toney, 2001)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Thiol-catalyzed formose route to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_010", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["thiol, e.g. 3-mercaptopropionic acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "313K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "thiol", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH about 5.2; weeks-long reaction in wet-dry shoreline pools; essentially no alanine is expected without the thiol promoter."}, "mechanism": "Formose-derived carbonyls undergo dehydration to methylglyoxal-like species, thiol trapping to hemithioacetals, imine chemistry with ammonia, and hydrolysis to alanine; lactate forms in parallel.", "reasoning": "Weber showed that thiol-catalyzed reactions of glyceraldehyde or of formaldehyde plus glycolaldehyde with ammonia generate alanine under mild aqueous conditions, with alanine appearing along with lactate and related products (Weber, 1985; Weber, 1998). This route avoids cyanide and connects sugar chemistry to amino-acid synthesis, but yields are low and the required thiol catalyst has its own prebiotic supply problem."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "CPL seed enrichment of the alanine pool", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["circularly polarized UV light"], "conditions": {"temperature": "280-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Solid film, dry residue, or ice-like surface layer; only percent-level enantiomeric excess is expected; helicity and wavelength must favor the L enantiomer."}, "mechanism": "Differential absorption and photodestruction of the two enantiomers biases the surviving alanine pool toward a small L excess.", "reasoning": "Circularly polarized UV light can induce small alanine enantiomeric excesses by differential photolysis or related photochemistry in solid-state and ice-analog experiments (Meinert et al., 2014; Modica et al., 2014). The effect is only percent-level and its direct terrestrial implementation is uncertain, so this step is treated only as a seed-enrichment module rather than a complete solution to the L problem."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Wet-dry amplification of a small L excess to L-alanine", "inputs": ["mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Repeated dissolution and recrystallization in evaporitic pools or tidal flats; requires a preexisting seed excess from rxn_011."}, "mechanism": "Repeated phase partitioning and selective crystallization amplify a preexisting slight L bias until an L-dominated alanine fraction is obtained.", "reasoning": "Speculative amplification module. No provided study directly demonstrates alanine-specific evaporative amplification to high L excess, but such a second-stage selection step is required because all experimentally supported synthesis routes are racemic and returned extraterrestrial samples also show alanine to be racemic (Glavin et al., 2021; Glavin et al., 2025). Wet-dry crystallization is included here as a chemically plausible, but unverified, way to amplify the small L excess from rxn_011."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Classical Strecker to L-alanine", "description": "Acetaldehyde, HCN, and ammonia form rac-alanine by classical Strecker chemistry; CPL then seeds a small L excess that is amplified during wet-dry crystallization.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Cyanosulfidic Strecker to L-alanine", "description": "A UV, HS-, Cu(I) surface network drives Strecker-type alanine formation from acetaldehyde, HCN, and ammonia, followed by the shared chiral-enrichment module.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs pyruvate route to L-alanine", "description": "Pyruvate, cyanide, ammonia, and CO2 generate alanine through Bucherer-Bergs chemistry, after which CPL and crystallization convert the racemate toward L-alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "FeS reductive amination route to L-alanine", "description": "Freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2 reductively aminates pyruvate in alkaline hydrothermal settings, producing alanine with lactate competition before the shared chiral module.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Green-rust route to L-alanine", "description": "Mixed-valence green rust converts pyruvate and ammonia to alanine under alkaline hydrothermal conditions, and the resulting alanine pool then enters CPL seeding and crystallization amplification.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_005", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Electro-reduced FeS vent route to L-alanine", "description": "Reduced FeS surfaces generated under vent-like electrochemical potentials aminatively reduce pyruvate to alanine, then the common two-step chiral module yields L-alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Ni0 and H2 route to L-alanine", "description": "Native nickel catalyzes hydrogenative amination of pyruvate with H2, producing alanine and some lactate before entry into the CPL-plus-crystallization module.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Hydroxylamine oxime-reduction route to L-alanine", "description": "Pyruvate reacts with hydroxylamine and is reduced by Fe0 through an oxime intermediate to alanine, which is then L-biased and amplified by the shared surface chiral module.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_008", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Pyridoxal transamination route to L-alanine", "description": "Aspartate transfers its amino group to pyruvate through Fe(III)-assisted pyridoxal chemistry, giving alanine and oxaloacetate before the common chiral-enrichment steps.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Thiol-formose route to L-alanine", "description": "Formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde react with ammonia in the presence of a thiol to produce alanine under mild surface conditions, after which the alanine pool is pushed toward L by CPL seeding and crystallization.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_011", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_013"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_004", "mol_012"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_004", "incoming_reactions": [], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_012", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_011"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_015", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_010"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 596775.0408649445, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T05:14:16.680212", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: The network separates alanine formation from chirality generation. Ten experimentally supported prebiotic routes converge on an alanine pool: two aldehyde/cyanide routes, one Bucherer-Bergs route, five pyruvate-to-alanine amination variants on Fe/Ni minerals or redox surfaces, one oxime-reduction route, one transamination route, and one thiol/formose route. Pyruvate is treated as a shared proximal C3 feedstock hub because the report identifies pyruvate supply, rather than pyruvate amination itself, as the main upstream bottleneck. Because all supported synthetic routes yield racemic alanine, the final conversion to L-alanine is modeled as an explicit two-step chiral module: circularly polarized UV photolysis seeds only a small L excess, and wet-dry crystallization then amplifies it in a deliberately speculative but chemically motivated final step. Lactate is included as the main competing sink where reduction outcompetes amination.\n\nMOLECULES (15 nodes):\n  - mol_001: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: C2H4O (acetaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_003: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: C3H4O3 (pyruvate) [starting]\n  - mol_005: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_006: H2 (hydrogen gas) [starting]\n  - mol_007: NH3O (hydroxylamine) [starting]\n  - mol_008: C4H7NO4 (aspartic acid) [starting]\n  - mol_009: C4H4O5 (oxaloacetate) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: CH2O (formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_011: C2H4O2 (glycolaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_012: C3H7NO2 (rac-alanine) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C3H7NO2 (L-enriched alanine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C3H7NO2 (L-alanine) [target]\n  - mol_015: C3H6O3 (lactate) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (12 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_012 (Classical Strecker synthesis of alanine from acetaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_001, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_012 (Cyanosulfidic Strecker synthesis of alanine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_001, mol_003, mol_004, mol_005 -> mol_012 (Bucherer-Bergs conversion of pyruvate to alanine) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_004: mol_003, mol_004 -> mol_012, mol_015 (FeS and ferrous hydroxide reductive amination of pyruvate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_005: mol_003, mol_004 -> mol_012, mol_015 (Green-rust reductive amination of pyruvate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_006: mol_003, mol_004 -> mol_012 (Electro-reduced FeS amination of pyruvate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_007: mol_003, mol_004, mol_006 -> mol_012, mol_015 (Ni0 and H2 reductive amination of pyruvate) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_008: mol_004, mol_007 -> mol_012 (Hydroxylamine and Fe0 oxime reduction of pyruvate) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_009: mol_004, mol_008 -> mol_009, mol_012 (Pyridoxal-metal transamination to alanine) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_010: mol_003, mol_010, mol_011 -> mol_012, mol_015 (Thiol-catalyzed formose route to alanine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_012 -> mol_013 (CPL seed enrichment of the alanine pool) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_013 -> mol_014 (Wet-dry amplification of a small L excess to L-alanine) [Surface]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Classical Strecker to L-alanine (3 steps)\n  - P2: Cyanosulfidic Strecker to L-alanine (3 steps)\n  - P3: Bucherer-Bergs pyruvate route to L-alanine (3 steps)\n  - P4: FeS reductive amination route to L-alanine (3 steps)\n  - P5: Green-rust route to L-alanine (3 steps)\n  - P6: Electro-reduced FeS vent route to L-alanine (3 steps)\n  - P7: Ni0 and H2 route to L-alanine (3 steps)\n  - P8: Hydroxylamine oxime-reduction route to L-alanine (3 steps)\n  - P9: Pyridoxal transamination route to L-alanine (3 steps)\n  - P10: Thiol-formose route to L-alanine (3 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 12 reactions | 3 balanced as written | 0 balanceable with coefficients | 9 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_001 UNBALANCED: HCN + C2H4O + NH3 -> C3H7NO2 | reactants minus products: H+1, N+1, O-1 | balanceable if adding: CO2 as reactant; O2 as reactant\n  - rxn_002 UNBALANCED: HCN + C2H4O + NH3 -> C3H7NO2 | reactants minus products: H+1, N+1, O-1 | balanceable if adding: CO2 as reactant; O2 as reactant\n  - rxn_003 UNBALANCED: HCN + NH3 + C3H4O3 + CO2 -> C3H7NO2 | reactants minus products: C+2, H+1, N+1, O+3 | balanceable if adding: CO2 as product\n  - rxn_004 UNBALANCED: NH3 + C3H4O3 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H6O3 | reactants minus products: C-3, H-6, O-2 | balanceable if adding: CO2 as product\n  - rxn_005 UNBALANCED: NH3 + C3H4O3 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H6O3 | reactants minus products: C-3, H-6, O-2 | balanceable if adding: CO2 as product\n  - rxn_006 UNBALANCED: NH3 + C3H4O3 -> C3H7NO2 | reactants minus products: O+1 | balanceable if adding: O2 as product\n  - rxn_007 UNBALANCED: NH3 + C3H4O3 + H2 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H6O3 | reactants minus products: C-3, H-4, O-2 | balanceable if adding: H2O as product; CO2 as product; O2 as product\n  - rxn_008 UNBALANCED: C3H4O3 + NH3O -> C3H7NO2 | reactants minus products: O+2 | balanceable if adding: O2 as product\n  - rxn_010 UNBALANCED: NH3 + CH2O + C2H4O2 -> C3H7NO2 + C3H6O3 | reactants minus products: C-3, H-4, O-2 | balanceable if adding: H2O as product\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Alanine: Routes, Evidence, Conditions and Contested Points\n\nRacemic alanine is one of the easiest proteinogenic amino acids to make abiotically. It has been synthesized from several unrelated feedstocks: HCN with acetaldehyde and NH3 (Strecker), pyruvate with NH3 and a reductant (reductive amination), pyruvate with cyanide, NH3 and CO2 (Bucherer\u2013Bergs), and aldehyde/sugar chemistry with NH3. It also arrives in meteorites. No experiment, however, makes the **L** enantiomer selectively. The real bottleneck for \"L-alanine\" is enantioenrichment, plus the problem of joining each route's feedstock synthesis to its amination step under one set of conditions. Which route is \"best\" depends on which framework you accept, and each is contested on yield, concentration, or geochemical plausibility.\n\n## TL;DR\n- **Two families of routes dominate, and both work in the lab.** (1) Aldehyde/cyanide chemistry: Strecker from acetaldehyde + HCN + NH3, starting with Miller's 1953 spark discharge, and today Sutherland's cyanosulfidic network. (2) Keto-acid chemistry: reductive amination of pyruvate on FeS, green rust, electro-reduced FeS, Fe0 or Ni0/H2. Pyruvate amination gives the highest single-step yields (~50\u201370%, \"nearly complete\" in some runs), but needs high NH3, alkaline pH and a pyruvate source that is itself low-yield (\u226410 \u00b5M from CO2 + H2).\n- **Every demonstrated route gives racemic alanine.** The only claimed natural L-excess, ~33% L-alanine in Murchison, was reported by Engel & Macko (1997, *Nature*) on the basis of \"nearly equal, extraterrestrial \u03b415N values measured for each alanine enantiomer\". It was disputed (Pizzarello & Cronin 1998). Later Murchison and Bennu analyses found alanine racemic. Asymmetric photolysis by circularly polarized light gives only percent-level excesses. Getting from racemic to L is unsolved for every framework.\n- **Environment is unresolved.** Alkaline hydrothermal/serpentinizing settings best fit the keto-acid routes, but face the \"hot-vent decomposition\" critique (Miller & Bada 1988). Surface UV/sulfidic or atmospheric-discharge settings fit the HCN routes, but depend on reducing gases or locally concentrated HCN/sulfide. For network design, treat pyruvate\u21c4alanine as a robust, multi-catalyst node. Treat the upstream C3 source and the chirality step as the open problems.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Route summary (evidence-weighted)**\n\n| Route | Feedstocks | Key intermediate(s) | Representative conditions | Best reported alanine outcome | Main critique |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Spark-discharge / Strecker | CH4, NH3, H2, H2O (or CO2/N2 with buffering) | HCN, acetaldehyde, \u03b1-aminopropionitrile | Electric discharge, then aqueous Strecker | Glycine 2.1% of CH4-carbon; alanine \u22480.54 mol per mol glycine (Miller) | Needs reducing atmosphere; neutral CO2/N2 atmospheres give much less unless nitrite is quenched |\n| Cyanosulfidic (Sutherland) | HCN, H2S/HS\u207b, Cu(I), UV, phosphate | Aldehydes from reductive HCN homologation; aminonitriles | 254 nm UV, sulfidic water | 12 amino acids accessed via Strecker | Local high HCN/sulfide; UV wavelength; stepwise \"one-pot\" claims |\n| Bucherer\u2013Bergs from \u03b1-ketoacids | Pyruvate, cyanide, NH3, CO2 | Hydantoin / N-carbamoyl amino acid | Aqueous, mild; CO2 essential | \u03b1-Amino acids from \u03b1-ketoacids | Still needs cyanide plus a pyruvate source |\n| Reductive amination on Fe minerals | Pyruvate, NH3, Fe(II)/Fe(III) minerals | Iminopyruvate | FeS/Fe(OH)2 at 50\u201375 \u00b0C; green rust 50\u201370 \u00b0C, alkaline | Up to ~50% (FeS); ~70% (green rust, pH 10, 70 \u00b0C, 72 h) | Requires 0.1\u20131.5 M NH3; lactate competes; stoichiometric mineral reductant |\n| Electro-reduced FeS / Fe0 | Pyruvate, NH3, H2 / electrochemical potential | Iminopyruvate | pH 9.6, 0.5 M NH3, 23\u201325 \u00b0C, 48 h | \"Nearly complete\" conversion | Requires a sustained negative potential in vents |\n| Ni0 / H2 | Pyruvate, NH3, H2 | Iminopyruvate | 22 \u00b0C, pH 7\u20138, 5 bar H2 | 18\u201340% (Ni or Pd/C); 17% at only 6 mM NH3 (Ni) | Native Ni availability; high H2 pressure |\n| Hydroxylamine + Fe0 | Pyruvate, NH2OH, Fe0 | Pyruvate oxime | Aqueous, mild | Alanine among 4 amino acids | NH2OH prebiotic availability |\n| Transamination | Pyruvate + another amino acid, pyridoxal, metal ions | Pyridoxamine / aldimine | Aqueous; Fe(III)/Al(III) cooperativity | Aspartate\u2192alanine works; pyridoxal raises Ni/H2 alanine from 7.6% to 34% | Pyridoxal's own prebiotic origin |\n| Thiol-catalyzed sugar route (Weber) | Glyceraldehyde, or HCHO + glycolaldehyde, NH3, thiol | Methylglyoxal-type dehydration products, hemithioacetal, imine | 40 \u00b0C, pH 5.2, 35 days | 1.8% (HCHO basis); 0.6% after 60 days (glyceraldehyde) | Low yield, slow; mercaptopropionic acid as catalyst |\n| Formose-type parent-body chemistry | HCHO, glycolaldehyde, acetaldehyde, NH3 | Aldimines / aminonitrile-free routes | 60\u2013250 \u00b0C aqueous | \u03b1-, \u03b2-, \u03b3-amino acids including alanine | Asteroidal rather than terrestrial; racemic |\n| Delivery / interstellar ice | CH3OH, NH3, H2O, CO ices + UV; parent-body chemistry | Radicals; Strecker precursors | Cryogenic UV; meteorite parent bodies | Racemic alanine (Bennu, ice residues) | Delivered amounts; survival on impact |\n\n## Details\n\n### 1. Strecker chemistry (HCN + acetaldehyde + NH3)\n\n**Chemistry.** CH3CHO + NH3 \u2192 imine. Adding HCN gives \u03b1-aminopropionitrile, and hydrolysis (via the amide) gives alanine. Strecker made alanine this way from acetaldehyde, ammonia and HCN in 1850, the first laboratory amino acid synthesis.\\[1\\] In Strecker chemistry the imine/aminonitrile and the cyanohydrin are in equilibrium until hydrolysis begins. Where NH3 is low or pH is low, aminonitriles partly convert to cyanohydrins, which hydrolyze to lactate rather than alanine (as noted in the 2021 *Science Advances* paper \"A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle\").\\[2\\]\n\n**Miller\u2013Urey evidence.** Miller (1953, *Science*) sparked CH4/NH3/H2/H2O and obtained glycine, alanine, \u03b2-alanine and \u03b1-aminobutyric acid.\\[3\\] Miller attributed this to Strecker chemistry: HCN and aldehydes formed in the gas phase reacted in the water.\\[3\\] Bada's retrospective (M\u00e8tode, citing Miller & Urey 1959) gives these yields relative to glycine = 1: alanine \u2248 0.54 mol, \u03b2-alanine \u2248 0.24 mol. From 710 mg of methane carbon, 4.8 mg glycine formed.\\[3\\]\\[4\\] Johnson et al. (2008, *Science*) give glycine at 2.1% of methane carbon for the classic apparatus, versus 1.8% and 0.4% for the other two.\\[5\\] Miller estimated that 50\u201360% of the methane carbon became organic compounds, mostly polymeric material.\\[3\\]\n\n**Variants.**\n- **H2S-rich atmosphere.** Parker et al. (2011, *PNAS*) reanalyzed a 1958 Miller experiment run with CH4, NH3, H2S and CO2. It produced 23 amino acids and 4 amines, with high relative abundances of alkyl amino acids including alanine.\\[6\\]\\[7\\]\n- **Neutral atmosphere.** Cleaves et al. (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*) showed that CO2/N2 atmospheres give far more amino acids than previously thought, including alanine, once nitrite/nitrate (which destroy amino acids during workup) are controlled and the solution is buffered. The alanine was racemic within experimental error.\\[8\\]\\[9\\]\n\n**Cyanosulfidic protometabolism (Sutherland school).** Patel et al. (2015, *Nature Chemistry*) showed that UV-driven (254 nm), HS\u207b/Cu-mediated reductive homologation of HCN produces precursors of ribonucleotides, lipids and amino acids. In that network 12 amino acids are reached by Strecker synthesis.\\[2\\]\\[10\\]\\[11\\] Alanine is fairly photostable in these systems. At high pH it photolyzes slowly (~6% products after 2.5 h), and NaSH suppresses its photodegradation. Slow oxidation of alanine to pyruvate was also observed (2021 *Sci. Adv.*).\\[2\\] This links the cyanide and keto-acid worlds.\n\n**Isotopic fingerprint.** Murchison alanine has a \u00b9\u00b3C-rich \u03b1-carbon, consistent with Strecker synthesis from acetaldehyde with a \u00b9\u00b3C-rich carbonyl carbon (Chimiak et al. 2020). Experiments then measured Strecker-specific isotope effects for alanine, e.g. a 55.1\u2030 nitrogen equilibrium isotope effect between NH3 and the aminonitrile (*Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*, 2022).\\[12\\] This is the strongest natural-sample evidence that Strecker chemistry actually made alanine somewhere in the early Solar System.\n\n**Critiques.**\n- The early atmosphere was probably weakly reducing or neutral (CO2/N2), which sharply lowers HCN and aldehyde production.\\[5\\]\n- The cyanosulfidic network needs locally high HCN and sulfide. Ranjan et al. (arXiv 1801.07725) analyzed sulfidic anion concentrations as an extra local constraint.\\[11\\]\n- Strecker chemistry is aldehyde-based. Extant biochemistry makes alanine from pyruvate. This \"disconnect\" is the main congruence critique raised by Pulletikurti et al. (2022).\\[13\\]\n\n### 2. Bucherer\u2013Bergs chemistry from pyruvate (cyanide + NH3 + CO2)\n\nPulletikurti, Yadav, Springsteen & Krishnamurthy (2022, *Nature Chemistry* 14:1142\u20131150) showed that \u03b1-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia sources to give the corresponding \u03b1-amino acids by the Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway (hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates).\\[14\\] The same chemistry gives dihydroorotate from oxaloacetate.\\[15\\] CO2 is mechanistically essential. Krishnamurthy: \"If you mix only the keto acid, cyanide and ammonia, it just sits there. As soon as you add carbon dioxide, even trace amounts, the reaction picks up speed.\"\\[16\\] This route is a bridge: it uses a cyanide feedstock but a metabolic (keto-acid) carbon skeleton.\\[13\\] Its weakness is that it still needs both a pyruvate source and cyanide in the same place. A related paper (Yadav et al. 2022, *Nature Chemistry*) showed cyanide acting as a reductant on TCA intermediates, but pyruvate itself was not reduced.\\[17\\]\\[18\\]\n\n### 3. Reductive amination of pyruvate (metabolism-first / hydrothermal frameworks)\n\n**Chemistry.** Pyruvate + NH3 + 2H\u207a + 2e\u207b \u2192 alanine + H2O, via iminopyruvate.\\[19\\] This is the same transformation alanine dehydrogenase catalyzes with NADH.\\[20\\] The universal competing reaction is reduction of pyruvate to lactate. Product selectivity depends on pH, NH3 concentration and the reductant.\n\n**Iron sulfide / ferrous hydroxide (W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user school).**\n- Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003, *Tetrahedron Letters* 44:1695\u20131697) reported that \"amino acids are formed efficiently by reductive amination of \u03b1-keto acids under aqueous conditions with freshly precipitated FeS or Fe(OH)2 and with NH3\u2026 at pH values near their pKa\" (pH ~9\u201310 for NH3).\\[21\\]\n- A secondary summary (Milner-White & Russell 2008, *Biology Direct*) quotes alanine, glutamate, phenylalanine and tyrosine yields up to ~50% at 50 and 75 \u00b0C.\\[22\\] The primary table was not verified here.\n- These experiments used large NH3 excesses. Kaur et al. (2024) characterize earlier Fe-based reductive aminations as using \"375 mM to 1.5 M\" ammonia.\\[23\\]\n- FeS acts as a stoichiometric reductant (oxidized toward pyrite/Fe(III)), not as a true catalyst.\\[19\\]\n\n**Mixed-valence iron hydroxides (green rust; Russell/Barge alkaline-vent school).** Barge et al. (2019, *PNAS* 116:4828\u20134833) reacted pyruvate + NH3 with Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxides:\\[24\\]\n- Alanine formed only when the mineral was partially oxidized. The maximum was at 1:1 Fe(II):Fe(III).\\[24\\]\n- Alkaline pH was required. At 75% Fe(II) and pH 7, \"no alanine was produced\", and lactate rose to 30\u201350% after 72 h. Lactate is favored at low pH.\n- The highest yield was at pH 10, Fe(II):Fe(III) = 1:1 and 70 \u00b0C: \"\u223c70%\" alanine with \"\u223c30% lactate\", relative to all organics detected in the liquid phase. At 50 \u00b0C \"the same reactions proceeded but more slowly.\"\n- Neither end member worked: \"Purely ferrous hydroxides did not drive reductive amination but instead promoted pyruvate reduction to lactate, and ferric hydroxides did not result in any reaction.\"\n- The authors place this in iron-rich sediments near alkaline vents, where redox and pH gradients would set alanine/lactate selectivity. They state that \"the precise mechanism of pyruvate reductive amination to alanine in these systems was not established.\"\\[24\\]\\[25\\]\n\n**Electro-reduced FeS and native metals (Kitadai/Nakamura).** Kitadai et al. (2019, *Science Advances* 5:eaav7848):\n- FeS was electrolyzed at \u22120.7 V (vs SHE) for 7 days, producing partial Fe0 (\"FeS_PERM\"). The authors argue such potentials arise naturally in H2-rich alkaline vents.\\[26\\]\n- At pH 9.6 (0.5 M NH3), 5 mM pyruvate and \u03b1-ketoglutarate \"reacted nearly completely\" to alanine and glutamate within 48 h at 23\u201325 \u00b0C.\\[26\\]\n- In a competitive one-pot run (four keto acids, 1.25 mM each), all amino acids formed in >50% yield, with alanine and glutamate favored.\\[27\\]\n- Pure Fe0 was less selective: it also made pyruvate and alanine by oxaloacetate decarboxylation.\\[27\\]\n\n**Ni0/H2 (Moran/Martin school).** Kaur et al. (2024, *Chem* 10:1528\u20131540):\n- Screening conditions: 30 mM pyruvate, 5 bar H2, 22 \u00b0C, pH 7\u20138, 24 h. Ni/SiO2\u2013Al2O3 or Pd/C gave 18\u201340% alanine with 5\u201315% lactate.\\[23\\]\n- At only 6 mM NH3, alanine reached up to 17% with Ni and 44% with Pd (relative to ammonia). This substantially relaxes the ammonia-concentration critique of the Fe-based work.\\[23\\]\n- The Aletai iron meteorite (18 mol% Ni) gave 24% alanine and 43% lactate.\\[23\\]\n- The system also runs a reverse-Krebs-cycle analog, making keto acids and amino acids in one pot.\\[28\\]\n- Follow-up work without H2 (Ni at 100 \u00b0C, pH 11, 18 h) aminated 6.4% of pyruvate to alanine.\\[29\\]\n\n**Hydroxylamine + Fe0.** Muchowska, Varma & Moran (2019, *Nature* 569:104\u2013107) showed an Fe\u00b2\u207a-promoted network in which pyruvate and glyoxylate build 9 of 11 TCA-cycle intermediates. Adding hydroxylamine and Fe0 gave four biological amino acids, including alanine, by oxime reduction \"in a manner that parallels biosynthesis\".\\[30\\] The prebiotic source of NH2OH is the weak link.\\[26\\]\n\n**Where does the pyruvate come from?** This is the upstream bottleneck for every keto-acid route.\n- Cody et al. (2000, *Science*) made pyruvate from formic acid with nonyl thiol and FeS at 250 \u00b0C and 200 MPa. W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user himself noted the paradox: pyruvate is thermally fragile (it decomposes near 165 \u00b0C).\\[31\\]\n- Preiner et al. (2020, *Nature Ecology & Evolution* 4:534\u2013542) showed greigite (Fe3S4), magnetite (Fe3O4) and awaruite (Ni3Fe) fixing CO2 with H2 at 100 \u00b0C under alkaline conditions. Their abstract reports \"formate (up to 200 mM), acetate (up to 100 \u00b5M), pyruvate (up to 10 \u00b5M), methanol (up to 100 \u00b5M) and methane\". Higher figures quoted in a 2023 *Frontiers in Microbiology* review (560 \u00b5M acetate, 0.3 M formate) do not match this primary abstract.\n- Huber, W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user and co-workers made pyruvate from acetylene + CO on NiS above 100 \u00b0C (2020, *Life*/PMC).\\[32\\]\n\nA tens-of-percent amination step fed by micromolar pyruvate gives micromolar alanine at best. Concentrating mechanisms (pores, thermophoresis, mineral adsorption) are invoked but not demonstrated for this specific pair.\n\n### 4. Transamination (pyridoxal and metal ions)\n\nPyridoxal (PL) transaminates amino acids without enzymes, and metal ions strongly modulate this.\\[33\\] Dherbassy, Mayer, Muchowska & Moran (2023, *JACS* 145:13357\u201313370) found cooperative metal\u2013pyridoxal catalysis, strongest with Fe(III) and Al(III) (roughly 180-fold over Fe(III) alone and 90-fold over pyridoxal alone, as quoted in a PMC review). They also found that the mechanism converges on the biological one.\\[34\\] Amino-group transfer from aspartate to pyruvate (giving alanine) proceeds readily, but alanine\u2192aspartate is poor (*Life* 2023, \"Prebiotic Synthesis of Aspartate Using Life's Metabolism as a Guide\").\\[35\\] In Ni/H2 systems (FEBS J), adding pyridoxal raised pyruvate\u2192alanine conversion from 7.6% to 34% at pH 11 and 80 \u00b0C, and up to 48% at 80\u2013100 \u00b0C. Pyridoxamine acts as the nitrogen shuttle and favors amination over lactate formation.\\[36\\] Counterpoint: Zabinski & Toney (2001, *JACS*) found that metal ions inhibit some PLP-catalyzed transaminations, so metal effects depend on the system.\\[37\\]\n\n### 5. Sugar/aldehyde routes without cyanide (Weber's \"triose model\")\n\nWeber (1985, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*) made alanine from glyceraldehyde and ammonium at ambient temperature. A thiol (3-mercaptopropionic acid) made the reaction about six times faster: 0.6% vs 0.1% after 60 days. The proposed path runs through dehydration of glyceraldehyde to a methylglyoxal-type species, a hemithioacetal and an imine, with lactate as a parallel product.\\[38\\] Weber (1998, *OLEB* 28:259\u2013270) then showed that formaldehyde + glycolaldehyde (formose substrates) + NH3 + thiol at pH 5.2 and 40 \u00b0C for 35 days gave 1.8% alanine (on formaldehyde), and essentially none without thiol.\\[39\\] Attractions: no HCN, a link to formose sugars, and thioester (activated) intermediates. Critiques: low yield, month-long timescales, and an organic thiol catalyst whose own prebiotic source must be explained.\n\n### 6. Parent-body and interstellar synthesis (delivery framework)\n\n- **Parent-body aqueous chemistry.** Kebukawa et al. (2017, *Science Advances*) heated formaldehyde, glycolaldehyde and ammonia (90\u2013250 \u00b0C) and made \u03b1-, \u03b2- and \u03b3-amino acids together with insoluble organic matter.\\[40\\] Koga & Naraoka (2017, *Scientific Reports*) made glycine, alanine and hydroxy amino acids from formaldehyde/acetaldehyde/NH3 at 60 \u00b0C and pH 11.5.\\[41\\] In their Murchison sample, \"the second most abundant amino acid was alanine (1.7 ppm) with a relative enrichment of L-alanine\". That L-enrichment conflicts with the racemic results discussed below.\n- **Interstellar ice photolysis.** UV-irradiated ice analogues give racemic amino acids, including alanine (Bernstein et al. 2002 and Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002, *Nature* 416).\n- **Returned samples.** Bennu samples (Glavin, Dworkin et al. 2025, *Nature Astronomy* 9:199\u2013210) contain 33 amino acids, 14 of them proteinogenic. The total is ~70 nmol/g, ~3.5\u00d7 less than Murchison, and alanine is racemic.\\[42\\]\\[43\\] The authors interpret the high glycine and racemic \u03b1-amino acids as consistent with HCN-polymerization/Strecker-type chemistry in an ammonia-rich parent body.\\[42\\]\n\nDelivery supplies alanine without terrestrial geochemical constraints. It is racemic, though, and quantitative impact survival and delivery fluxes are uncertain.\n\n### 7. Thermodynamics and stability (hydrothermal frameworks)\n\nAmend & Shock (1998, *Science* 281:1659\u20131662) calculated that net alanine synthesis from CO2, NH4\u207a and H2 in a 100 \u00b0C mixed hydrothermal solution releases 12.12 kJ/mol. Synthesis is exergonic there but endergonic in cold oxidized surface seawater.\\[44\\] This supports the idea that H2-rich hydrothermal mixing zones thermodynamically favor alanine. The kinetic counterpoint: Aubrey, Cleaves & Bada (2009, *OLEB*) found that at 175 \u00b0C glycine and alanine peak after ~75 h and then decompose by deamination/decarboxylation.\\[45\\] Miller & Bada (1988, *Nature* 334:609\u2013611) argued that vent temperatures \"would not allow synthesis of organic compounds, but would decompose them, unless the exposure time at vent temperatures was short.\"\\[46\\] Note that the most productive keto-acid experiments (Barge, Kitadai, Kaur) run at 22\u201370 \u00b0C, well below black-smoker temperatures. That partly answers the critique for off-axis alkaline systems, but not for high-temperature (>150 \u00b0C) pyruvate formation steps like Cody et al.\n\n### 8. The \"L\" problem\n\nAll routes above give racemic alanine. The evidence on natural enantioenrichment:\n- **Murchison.** Engel & Macko (1997, *Nature* 389:265\u2013268) reported an L-alanine excess ([L \u2212 D]/[L + D]) of ~33%. They argued it was extraterrestrial on the basis of \"nearly equal, extraterrestrial \u03b415N values measured for each alanine enantiomer\". Pizzarello & Cronin (1998, *Nature* 394:236) found interior Murchison alanine racemic. They attributed the excess to exterior contamination or incomplete chromatographic resolution.\\[47\\] Glavin et al. (2021, *Meteoritics & Planetary Science*) found Murchison alanine \"both racemic (D \u2248 L) and heavily enriched in 13C, indicating no measurable terrestrial alanine contamination\" (16.5 nmol of racemic alanine in a 1 g extract). In the same samples glutamic acid showed ~15\u201339% L-excess.\n- **Bennu.** Alanine is racemic (2025).\\[48\\]\\[49\\]\n- **Circularly polarized light.** Asymmetric photolysis of racemic alanine films (Meinert et al. 2014, *Angewandte Chemie*) and CPL irradiation of ice analogues (Modica et al. 2014, *ApJ* 788:79; up to |1.34|% ee) induce only small, helicity- and wavelength-dependent excesses.\\[50\\]\\[51\\]\\[52\\]\\[53\\]\n\nSo no published route produces L-alanine selectively. An amplification mechanism (crystallization, autocatalysis, chiral templating, or later selection by chiral catalysts) has to be added to any route.\\[54\\] This is common to all frameworks and is not specific to alanine.\n\n## Catalysts and surfaces: what is actually shown\n\n- **No catalyst needed:** the Strecker steps (imine formation, cyanide addition, nitrile hydrolysis under acid or base) and Bucherer\u2013Bergs (needs CO2/bicarbonate as reagent, not a mineral catalyst). Spark discharge supplies the energy for HCN and aldehyde formation.\n- **Mineral or metal reductants/catalysts for pyruvate \u2192 alanine:**\n  - freshly precipitated FeS and Fe(OH)2 (stoichiometric)\n  - mixed-valence green rust (Fe(II):Fe(III) \u2248 1:1)\n  - electro-reduced FeS / Fe0\n  - native Fe0 with NH2OH\n  - Ni0 or Pd with H2 (catalytic in metal, H2 as reductant)\n  - Ni-bearing meteorite metal\n- **Upstream C\u2013C bond formation (CO2 \u2192 pyruvate):** greigite, magnetite, awaruite (with H2); FeS with thiols at high T/P; NiS with CO/acetylene.\n- **Organocatalysts/cofactors:** thiols (Weber); pyridoxal with Fe(III)/Al(III) (transamination); Cu(I)/HS\u207b photoredox (cyanosulfidic).\n\n## Recommendations for synthesis-network design\n\n1. **Model pyruvate \u21c4 alanine as a reversible, multi-catalyst hub.** It is the single best-supported step across frameworks: Fe minerals, Ni/H2, NH2OH/Fe0, pyridoxal transamination, and Bucherer\u2013Bergs with cyanide. Always include lactate as the competing sink. Make selectivity a function of pH (alkaline favors alanine), NH3 activity and reductant redox state.\n2. **Treat the C3 source as the rate-limiting, framework-defining choice.** Use either acetaldehyde + HCN (Strecker; needs a reducing atmosphere or local HCN/sulfide) or CO2/H2 \u2192 pyruvate (\u00b5M yields on Fe/Ni minerals). Do not assume high pyruvate concentrations without an explicit concentrating mechanism.\n3. **Use realistic NH3.** Fe-mineral yields come from 0.1\u20131.5 M NH3. Only the Ni/H2 (Kaur 2024) and pyridoxal-shuttle data show useful yields near 5\u20136 mM. Flag any pathway that needs molar NH3.\n4. **Keep amination steps \u2264~100 \u00b0C.** The best amination data are at 22\u201375 \u00b0C. Above ~150 \u00b0C, alanine decomposes on timescales of days.\n5. **Add an explicit, separate enantioenrichment module.** No feedstock route provides it, and claims of natural L-alanine excess are not reliable.\n\n## Caveats and contested points\n\n- **Hydrothermal vs surface.** Contested. Vent chemistry has thermodynamic support (Amend & Shock) and mild-temperature experimental support (Barge, Kitadai, Kaur). It is still criticized for dilution, decomposition at high T, and reliance on lab-prepared minerals, electrochemical potentials or 5 bar H2. Surface cyanide chemistry is criticized for needing reducing atmospheres or local HCN/sulfide/UV, and for incongruence with keto-acid biochemistry.\n- **Yields not verified against primary tables.** The Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user (2003) ~50% figure is from a secondary review. Kitadai's alanine is described as \"nearly complete\" (exact percentage in their Table S1). Miller's alanine figure is given relative to glycine; the commonly quoted absolute value (~1.7% of carbon) was not confirmed from the 1955 *JACS* table.\n- **Yield definitions differ** (percent of keto acid, of NH3, of carbon), so cross-route comparisons in the table are indicative only.\n- **The NASA/JPL press release** on Barge et al. described the chemistry as \"reproducing origins of life on the ocean floor\"; that framing outruns the data, which show one reaction step.\\[55\\]\n\n## Sources\n\n1. [The 1953 Stanley L. Miller Experiment: Fifty Years of Prebiotic ...](https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1023/A:1024807125069.pdf)\n2. [A cyanosulfidic origin of the Krebs cycle](https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.abh3981)\n3. [One of the foremost experiments of the 20th century - Revista M\u00e8tode](https://metode.org/issues/monographs/one-of-the-foremost-experiments-of-the-20th-century.html)\n4. [M\u00e8tode Science Studies Journal ISSN: 2174-3487 metodessj@uv.es](https://www.redalyc.org/pdf/5117/511754471026.pdf)\n5. <https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Johnsonetal2008.pdf>\n6. [Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 ...](https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/691/analytical/PDF/Parker2011.pdf)\n7. [PREBIOTIC CHEMISTRY Enhanced Synthesis of Alkyl Amino Acids in Miller\u2019s](https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Parker2011b.pdf)\n8. [PREBIOTIC CHEMISTRY A Reassessment of Prebiotic Organic Synthesis in Neutral](https://www.bio.miami.edu/dana/dox/Cleaves2008.pdf)\n9. [The first peptides: the evolutionary transition between prebiotic amino acids and early proteins](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0909.2832)\n10. [Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism.](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Common-origins-of-RNA,-protein-and-lipid-precursors-Patel-Percivalle/3b482858daca31713acd680830b446c96e0ee8d5)\n11. [Sulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth for Surficial Origins-of-Life Chemistry](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1801.07725)\n12. [Isotope effects at the origin of life: Fingerprints of the Strecker synthesis - ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0016703722000266)\n13. [(PDF) Cyanide as a Primordial Reductant enables a Protometabolic Reductive Glyoxylate Pathway](https://www.researchgate.net/publicati