\"C4H6N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-methylimidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"5-methylhydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alanine (rac/unspecified)\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Mineral-assisted CO2/H2 fixation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"greigite (Fe3S4)\", \"magnetite (Fe3O4)\", \"awaruite (Ni3Fe)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"greigite/magnetite/awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"alkaline, H2-rich vent fluid; pyruvate observed at only micromolar levels\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-mediated CO2 reduction to C1/C2 species followed by mineral-promoted C-C coupling to the alpha-keto acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preiner et al. (2020) detected pyruvate, along with formate and acetate, from CO2/H2 on greigite, magnetite, and awaruite under alkaline hydrothermal conditions. Amend and Shock (1998) also showed that amino-acid synthesis is thermodynamically favored in H2-rich hydrothermal systems. The main limitation is yield: pyruvate is only a low-abundance product, so this node is treated as the upstream bottleneck rather than assumed to supply concentrated pyruvate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"NiS-mediated acetylene/CO synthesis of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiS\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"CO-rich hydrothermal setting; short residence times favored because pyruvate is thermally fragile\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"NiS-bound acetylene undergoes carbonylation and hydration to give the C3 alpha-keto acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Huber et al. (2020) reported pyruvate formation from acetylene and CO on NiS at temperatures above 100 C. This route can bypass the very low yields of direct CO2/H2 pyruvate formation, but it inherits the thermal-fragility critique for pyruvate and amino acids at elevated temperature emphasized by Miller and Bada (1988).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Green-rust reductive amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"green rust (mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-343K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate-high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"green rust\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH about 10, Fe(II):Fe(III) about 1:1, high NH3 activity; lactate competes strongly at lower pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvate first forms an imine or iminopyruvate with ammonia, then the surface provides the reducing equivalents needed to hydrogenate the C=N intermediate to alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Barge et al. (2019) found the strongest alanine selectivity in mixed-valence iron hydroxides, with about 70% alanine at pH 10 and 70 C and much higher lactate production at lower pH or less favorable redox state. Their laboratory system likely used the mineral as the immediate reductant, so representing the step with H2 is an explicit geochemical abstraction that captures vent reducing power while preserving elemental balance.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Electroreduced FeS reductive amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"electroreduced FeS (FeS_PERM)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate-high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"electroreduced FeS (FeS_PERM)\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH 9.6, about 0.5 M NH3, sustained negative potential analogous to alkaline vent redox gradients\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine with pyruvate and the reduced FeS surface supplies electrons for reductive amination.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kitadai et al. (2019) showed that electroreduced FeS converted pyruvate to alanine nearly completely within 48 h at pH 9.6 with high ammonia. The experimentally critical driver is a sustained negative potential; H2 is included here only as the balanced thermodynamic equivalent of that reducing power. The geochemical availability of such persistent potentials remains an open systems question.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Ni0/H2 reductive amination of pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"native nickel (Ni0)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni0\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH 7-8; useful conversion even at millimolar NH3; lactate remains a side product\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nickel catalyzes hydrogenation of the pyruvate-derived imine or iminopyruvate to alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) reported 18-40% alanine from pyruvate under H2 with Ni catalysts and still appreciable conversion at about 6 mM NH3, relaxing the high-ammonia critique of older Fe-mineral systems. This is the cleanest experimentally supported balanced pyruvate-to-alanine route in the network, although native Ni availability and sustained H2 pressure are still caveats.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs cyclization of pyruvate to 5-methylhydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"cyanide/ammonia aqueous pool with dissolved CO2/HCO3- present and mechanistically essential, though not net consumed in this simplified step\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the keto acid and the resulting adduct cyclizes through Bucherer-Bergs chemistry to the hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pulletikurti et al. (2022) showed that alpha-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia sources through Bucherer-Bergs chemistry to hydantoin or N-carbamoyl intermediates en route to alpha-amino acids. Their study and the associated discussion identified dissolved CO2 as mechanistically essential, so CO2 is treated here as an indispensable condition even though the simplified balanced step does not consume it net.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"mild aqueous hydrolysis of the hydantoin pool\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ring opening and hydrolysis of the hydantoin release alanine together with CO2 and NH3.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the downstream hydrolysis step of the Bucherer-Bergs pathway documented by Pulletikurti et al. (2022). It is chemically important because it links a cyanide-enabled route to a metabolic carbon skeleton, but it still requires pyruvate and cyanide to become co-localized in the same aqueous setting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker aminonitrile formation from acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"HCN- and aldehyde-rich aqueous surface setting; compatible with spark-discharge or cyanosulfidic feedstock supply\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetaldehyde forms an imine with ammonia and cyanide adds to that imine to generate the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the canonical first half of Strecker synthesis. Prebiotic settings that supply acetaldehyde and HCN include spark-discharge chemistry (Miller, 1953; Parker et al., 2011; Cleaves et al., 2008) and cyanosulfidic surface networks (Patel et al., 2015). Encoding the aminonitrile explicitly avoids overcompressing the Strecker route and keeps the mass balance correct.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminopropionitrile hydrolysis to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"neutral to mildly alkaline hydrolysis; low NH3 or low pH would favor cyanohydrin/lactate side chemistry\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The nitrile hydrates stepwise through amide chemistry to the amino acid, liberating ammonia overall.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminonitrile hydrolysis is the canonical second half of Strecker synthesis and is supported both by classical chemistry and by isotope evidence linking meteoritic alanine to Strecker-like formation (Chimiak et al., 2022). Patel et al. (2015) and later cyanosulfidic work also emphasize that aminonitrile versus cyanohydrin partitioning is sensitive to ammonia activity and pH, which is why neutral-to-alkaline conditions are favored here.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiol-assisted formose/aldehyde synthesis of alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"thiol catalyst (for example 3-mercaptopropionic acid)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"thiol catalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH about 5.2, weeks-long reaction, low yield\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formose-derived carbonyls pass through glyceraldehyde or methylglyoxal-like intermediates, thiol capture, and imine chemistry before hydrolysis gives alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Weber (1985) and Weber (1998) obtained alanine from glyceraldehyde or from formaldehyde plus glycolaldehyde plus NH3 in water only when a thiol catalyst was present; the route is real but slow and low yielding. Related aldehyde-ammonia chemistry also appears in parent-body aqueous systems (Kebukawa et al., 2017; Koga and Naraoka, 2017). This node is therefore included as a cyanide-free alternative, with the low yield and the prebiotic availability of the organic thiol explicitly acknowledged as weaknesses.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface enantioenrichment to L-alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"false\",\n        \"other\": \"dry film or evaporative residue under far-UV circularly polarized irradiation, followed by repeated wet-dry recrystallization; only small ee is experimentally demonstrated\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Asymmetric photolysis or photochemistry creates a small enantiomeric excess that is then hypothetically amplified by selective recrystallization or related physical sorting.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No published prebiotic synthesis makes alanine with substantial L-selectivity; alanine in meteorites is generally racemic and the earlier Murchison L-excess claim is disputed (Pizzarello and Cronin, 1998; Glavin et al., 2021; Glavin et al., 2025). Circularly polarized UV gives only percent-level alanine enantiomeric excess (Meinert et al., 2014; Modica et al., 2014), so this node is explicitly speculative and represents a small asymmetric bias plus unresolved amplification rather than a demonstrated high-yield synthetic step.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2/H2 fixation plus green-rust amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation first makes pyruvate, green rust aminates it to rac-alanine, and a speculative surface enrichment module then biases the product toward L-alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2/H2 fixation plus electroreduced FeS amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation makes pyruvate, electroreduced FeS converts it to rac-alanine under alkaline vent-like redox conditions, and surface enrichment supplies the L bias.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2/H2 fixation plus Ni/H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate produced from CO2/H2 is aminated on native nickel under H2 and then enters the common surface enrichment step to reach L-alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2/H2 fixation plus Bucherer-Bergs bridge route\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 chemistry supplies pyruvate, which is trapped in a cyanide-bearing prebiotic pool as a hydantoin and hydrolyzed to rac-alanine before speculative surface enrichment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene/CO pyruvate route plus green-rust amination\",\n      \"description\": \"NiS-mediated hydrothermal acetylene/CO chemistry furnishes pyruvate, green rust converts it to rac-alanine, and surface enrichment biases the product toward L-alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene/CO pyruvate route plus electroreduced FeS amination\",\n      \"description\": \"A NiS hydrothermal route generates pyruvate, electroreduced FeS aminates it under alkaline reducing conditions, and a later surface step provides the only encoded L bias.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene/CO pyruvate route plus Ni/H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal acetylene/CO chemistry yields pyruvate and H2, native nickel uses the reducing environment to give rac-alanine, and surface enrichment gives the target enantiomer.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene/CO pyruvate route plus Bucherer-Bergs bridge route\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate from NiS-mediated acetylene/CO chemistry enters a cyanide/ammonia hydantoin pathway and is then hydrolyzed to rac-alanine before speculative surface enrichment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface Strecker route via aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde, HCN, and NH3 form aminopropionitrile in a Strecker step, hydrolysis gives rac-alanine, and a final surface enrichment module provides L-alanine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiol-assisted formose/aldehyde route\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde react with ammonia in a thiol-assisted surface network to give rac-alanine directly, which then undergoes speculative surface enantioenrichment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_012\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-Alanine", "overall_strategy": "Parallel literature-supported routes are encoded rather than a single one-pot scenario. Pyruvate is used as the main hub because pyruvate-to-alanine is the best-supported experimental transformation; two hydrothermal C3-source modules feed four pyruvate-to-rac-alanine modules, giving eight pathways. Classical Strecker chemistry and a thiol-assisted formose/aldehyde route provide two independent surface alternatives. All feedstock routes produce rac-alanine, so a final explicit, weakly supported surface enantioenrichment module is included to reach L-alanine; lactate competition is acknowledged in the pyruvate-amination reasoning rather than expanded as a separate node.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CO", "iupac_name": "carbon monoxide", "common_name": "carbon monoxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "ethyne", "common_name": "acetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "alpha-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6N2/c1-3(4)2-5/h3H,4H2,1H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H6N2O2", "iupac_name": "5-methylimidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "5-methylhydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "alanine (rac/unspecified)", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "glycolaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Mineral-assisted CO2/H2 fixation to pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["greigite (Fe3S4)", "magnetite (Fe3O4)", "awaruite (Ni3Fe)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-380K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "greigite/magnetite/awaruite", "aqueous": "true", "other": "alkaline, H2-rich vent fluid; pyruvate observed at only micromolar levels"}, "mechanism": "Surface-mediated CO2 reduction to C1/C2 species followed by mineral-promoted C-C coupling to the alpha-keto acid.", "reasoning": "Preiner et al. (2020) detected pyruvate, along with formate and acetate, from CO2/H2 on greigite, magnetite, and awaruite under alkaline hydrothermal conditions. Amend and Shock (1998) also showed that amino-acid synthesis is thermodynamically favored in H2-rich hydrothermal systems. The main limitation is yield: pyruvate is only a low-abundance product, so this node is treated as the upstream bottleneck rather than assumed to supply concentrated pyruvate."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "NiS-mediated acetylene/CO synthesis of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_004", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["NiS"], "conditions": {"temperature": "390-430K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "NiS", "aqueous": "true", "other": "CO-rich hydrothermal setting; short residence times favored because pyruvate is thermally fragile"}, "mechanism": "NiS-bound acetylene undergoes carbonylation and hydration to give the C3 alpha-keto acid.", "reasoning": "Huber et al. (2020) reported pyruvate formation from acetylene and CO on NiS at temperatures above 100 C. This route can bypass the very low yields of direct CO2/H2 pyruvate formation, but it inherits the thermal-fragility critique for pyruvate and amino acids at elevated temperature emphasized by Miller and Bada (1988)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Green-rust reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["green rust (mixed Fe(II)/Fe(III) hydroxide)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-343K", "pressure": "moderate-high", "catalyst": "green rust", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH about 10, Fe(II):Fe(III) about 1:1, high NH3 activity; lactate competes strongly at lower pH"}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate first forms an imine or iminopyruvate with ammonia, then the surface provides the reducing equivalents needed to hydrogenate the C=N intermediate to alanine.", "reasoning": "Barge et al. (2019) found the strongest alanine selectivity in mixed-valence iron hydroxides, with about 70% alanine at pH 10 and 70 C and much higher lactate production at lower pH or less favorable redox state. Their laboratory system likely used the mineral as the immediate reductant, so representing the step with H2 is an explicit geochemical abstraction that captures vent reducing power while preserving elemental balance."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Electroreduced FeS reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["electroreduced FeS (FeS_PERM)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-300K", "pressure": "moderate-high", "catalyst": "electroreduced FeS (FeS_PERM)", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH 9.6, about 0.5 M NH3, sustained negative potential analogous to alkaline vent redox gradients"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine with pyruvate and the reduced FeS surface supplies electrons for reductive amination.", "reasoning": "Kitadai et al. (2019) showed that electroreduced FeS converted pyruvate to alanine nearly completely within 48 h at pH 9.6 with high ammonia. The experimentally critical driver is a sustained negative potential; H2 is included here only as the balanced thermodynamic equivalent of that reducing power. The geochemical availability of such persistent potentials remains an open systems question."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Ni0/H2 reductive amination of pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["native nickel (Ni0)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-300K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni0", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH 7-8; useful conversion even at millimolar NH3; lactate remains a side product"}, "mechanism": "Nickel catalyzes hydrogenation of the pyruvate-derived imine or iminopyruvate to alanine.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) reported 18-40% alanine from pyruvate under H2 with Ni catalysts and still appreciable conversion at about 6 mM NH3, relaxing the high-ammonia critique of older Fe-mineral systems. This is the cleanest experimentally supported balanced pyruvate-to-alanine route in the network, although native Ni availability and sustained H2 pressure are still caveats."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs cyclization of pyruvate to 5-methylhydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "cyanide/ammonia aqueous pool with dissolved CO2/HCO3- present and mechanistically essential, though not net consumed in this simplified step"}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the keto acid and the resulting adduct cyclizes through Bucherer-Bergs chemistry to the hydantoin.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti et al. (2022) showed that alpha-ketoacids react with cyanide and ammonia sources through Bucherer-Bergs chemistry to hydantoin or N-carbamoyl intermediates en route to alpha-amino acids. Their study and the associated discussion identified dissolved CO2 as mechanistically essential, so CO2 is treated here as an indispensable condition even though the simplified balanced step does not consume it net."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydantoin hydrolysis to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002", "mol_007"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "mild aqueous hydrolysis of the hydantoin pool"}, "mechanism": "Ring opening and hydrolysis of the hydantoin release alanine together with CO2 and NH3.", "reasoning": "This is the downstream hydrolysis step of the Bucherer-Bergs pathway documented by Pulletikurti et al. (2022). It is chemically important because it links a cyanide-enabled route to a metabolic carbon skeleton, but it still requires pyruvate and cyanide to become co-localized in the same aqueous setting."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Strecker aminonitrile formation from acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_007", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "HCN- and aldehyde-rich aqueous surface setting; compatible with spark-discharge or cyanosulfidic feedstock supply"}, "mechanism": "Acetaldehyde forms an imine with ammonia and cyanide adds to that imine to generate the alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the canonical first half of Strecker synthesis. Prebiotic settings that supply acetaldehyde and HCN include spark-discharge chemistry (Miller, 1953; Parker et al., 2011; Cleaves et al., 2008) and cyanosulfidic surface networks (Patel et al., 2015). Encoding the aminonitrile explicitly avoids overcompressing the Strecker route and keeps the mass balance correct."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Aminopropionitrile hydrolysis to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "neutral to mildly alkaline hydrolysis; low NH3 or low pH would favor cyanohydrin/lactate side chemistry"}, "mechanism": "The nitrile hydrates stepwise through amide chemistry to the amino acid, liberating ammonia overall.", "reasoning": "Aminonitrile hydrolysis is the canonical second half of Strecker synthesis and is supported both by classical chemistry and by isotope evidence linking meteoritic alanine to Strecker-like formation (Chimiak et al., 2022). Patel et al. (2015) and later cyanosulfidic work also emphasize that aminonitrile versus cyanohydrin partitioning is sensitive to ammonia activity and pH, which is why neutral-to-alkaline conditions are favored here."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Thiol-assisted formose/aldehyde synthesis of alanine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["thiol catalyst (for example 3-mercaptopropionic acid)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "313K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "thiol catalyst", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH about 5.2, weeks-long reaction, low yield"}, "mechanism": "Formose-derived carbonyls pass through glyceraldehyde or methylglyoxal-like intermediates, thiol capture, and imine chemistry before hydrolysis gives alanine.", "reasoning": "Weber (1985) and Weber (1998) obtained alanine from glyceraldehyde or from formaldehyde plus glycolaldehyde plus NH3 in water only when a thiol catalyst was present; the route is real but slow and low yielding. Related aldehyde-ammonia chemistry also appears in parent-body aqueous systems (Kebukawa et al., 2017; Koga and Naraoka, 2017). This node is therefore included as a cyanide-free alternative, with the low yield and the prebiotic availability of the organic thiol explicitly acknowledged as weaknesses."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Surface enantioenrichment to L-alanine", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "280-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "false", "other": "dry film or evaporative residue under far-UV circularly polarized irradiation, followed by repeated wet-dry recrystallization; only small ee is experimentally demonstrated"}, "mechanism": "Asymmetric photolysis or photochemistry creates a small enantiomeric excess that is then hypothetically amplified by selective recrystallization or related physical sorting.", "reasoning": "No published prebiotic synthesis makes alanine with substantial L-selectivity; alanine in meteorites is generally racemic and the earlier Murchison L-excess claim is disputed (Pizzarello and Cronin, 1998; Glavin et al., 2021; Glavin et al., 2025). Circularly polarized UV gives only percent-level alanine enantiomeric excess (Meinert et al., 2014; Modica et al., 2014), so this node is explicitly speculative and represents a small asymmetric bias plus unresolved amplification rather than a demonstrated high-yield synthetic step."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "CO2/H2 fixation plus green-rust amination", "description": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation first makes pyruvate, green rust aminates it to rac-alanine, and a speculative surface enrichment module then biases the product toward L-alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "CO2/H2 fixation plus electroreduced FeS amination", "description": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation makes pyruvate, electroreduced FeS converts it to rac-alanine under alkaline vent-like redox conditions, and surface enrichment supplies the L bias.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "CO2/H2 fixation plus Ni/H2 amination", "description": "Pyruvate produced from CO2/H2 is aminated on native nickel under H2 and then enters the common surface enrichment step to reach L-alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "CO2/H2 fixation plus Bucherer-Bergs bridge route", "description": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 chemistry supplies pyruvate, which is trapped in a cyanide-bearing prebiotic pool as a hydantoin and hydrolyzed to rac-alanine before speculative surface enrichment.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Acetylene/CO pyruvate route plus green-rust amination", "description": "NiS-mediated hydrothermal acetylene/CO chemistry furnishes pyruvate, green rust converts it to rac-alanine, and surface enrichment biases the product toward L-alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Acetylene/CO pyruvate route plus electroreduced FeS amination", "description": "A NiS hydrothermal route generates pyruvate, electroreduced FeS aminates it under alkaline reducing conditions, and a later surface step provides the only encoded L bias.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Acetylene/CO pyruvate route plus Ni/H2 amination", "description": "Hydrothermal acetylene/CO chemistry yields pyruvate and H2, native nickel uses the reducing environment to give rac-alanine, and surface enrichment gives the target enantiomer.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Acetylene/CO pyruvate route plus Bucherer-Bergs bridge route", "description": "Pyruvate from NiS-mediated acetylene/CO chemistry enters a cyanide/ammonia hydantoin pathway and is then hydrolyzed to rac-alanine before speculative surface enrichment.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_011", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Surface Strecker route via aminopropionitrile", "description": "Acetaldehyde, HCN, and NH3 form aminopropionitrile in a Strecker step, hydrolysis gives rac-alanine, and a final surface enrichment module provides L-alanine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_012"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Thiol-assisted formose/aldehyde route", "description": "Formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde react with ammonia in a thiol-assisted surface network to give rac-alanine directly, which then undergoes speculative surface enantioenrichment.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_006", "mol_012"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_i