n only naming electroreduced FeS.\",\n        \"Replace rxn_011 with an explicit partial ee mechanism.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.69,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.84,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.18\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.63,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.78,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.58,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.31,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.59,\n      \"feedback\": \"Probably the strongest hydrothermal chain in the network: both the NiS acetylene/CO pyruvate step and Ni/H2 amination have experimental support. It still needs explicit NiS and 5 bar scale H2 conditions and does not solve the final L-alanine problem.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct rxn_002 stoichiometry and define the NiS temperature and residence time assumptions.\",\n        \"Annotate rxn_005 with realistic H2 pressure, Ni source, pH, and NH3 concentration.\",\n        \"Treat enantioenrichment as a separate low-yield surface selection step, not complete conversion.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.69,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.60,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.71,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.18\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.54,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.69,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.44,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.30,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.51,\n      \"feedback\": \"A plausible bridge route on paper, but the Bucherer-Bergs step needs CO2 or bicarbonate in the conditions and the hydrolysis stoichiometry should be corrected. The route also jumps from hot hydrothermal pyruvate generation to a vague Biochemical module and then to surface enantioenrichment without transport justification.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct rxn_002 stoichiometry and specify the NiS hydrothermal conditions.\",\n        \"State the CO2 or bicarbonate requirement for rxn_006 and correct rxn_007 to use 2 H2O.\",\n        \"Make the transfer from hydrothermal to surface settings explicit and weaken rxn_011 to partial enrichment.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.81,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.18\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.67,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.84,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.79,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.34,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.67,\n      \"feedback\": \"Best-aligned pathway. Classical Strecker formation of alpha-aminopropionitrile and subsequent hydrolysis to alanine are robust literature chemistry and fit a surface aqueous setting. The remaining weakness is that rxn_009 should consume 2 H2O and the final step does not yet provide a credible route from rac-alanine to L-alanine.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct rxn_009 to aminopropionitrile + 2 H2O -> alanine + NH3 and specify hydrolysis conditions.\",\n        \"If desired, add an upstream HCN or acetaldehyde generation module instead of treating both as unrestricted starts.\",\n        \"Replace rxn_011 with a quantified small-ee enrichment or resolution mechanism.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.58,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.18\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.42,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.58,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.64,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.31,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.48,\n      \"feedback\": \"Weber-type aldehyde chemistry is real but low-yield, slow, and thiol-dependent. Because the named thiol catalyst is not represented and the final enantioenrichment is still speculative, this route is weaker than the Strecker module.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Add the thiol catalyst explicitly to rxn_010 or at least annotate it as an essential condition.\",\n        \"State the low-yield, long-time, mildly acidic conditions for the Weber route.\",\n        \"Model the last step as partial enantioenrichment rather than complete conversion to L-alanine.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.64,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"Connected and mostly internally consistent network: RDKit reports 0 unbalanceable reactions, but rxn_001 should be 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvate + 3 H2O, rxn_002 should be C2H2 + CO + 2 H2O -> pyruvate + H2, and rxn_007 and rxn_009 each need 2 H2O; mol_010 also needs a repairable structure or InChI. Literature alignment is good for the rac-alanine chemistry: Strecker, pyruvate reductive amination, Bucherer-Bergs, and Weber-style aldehyde chemistry are all represented and the network explicitly notes lactate competition, low pyruvate supply, and the chirality bottleneck. The main reason confidence stays moderate is rxn_011; current literature does not support a robust prebiotic full conversion of rac-alanine to L-alanine, only weak or contested small-ee scenarios. Additional issues are the missing CO2 or bicarbonate context for rxn_006, omission of the thiol catalyst in rxn_010, and lack of explicit transport and concentration steps from hydrothermal modules to a surface enrichment setting. Best current pathway is P9, but overall this is a strong modular rac-alanine network rather than a demonstrated L-alanine synthesis network, so it is not approved.\"\n}",