mers in the Murchison meteorite (https://www.nature.com/articles/28306)\n  45. The science from asteroid sample return missions (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2604.22182)\n  46. EXTRATERRESTRIAL AMINO ACIDS IDENTIFIED IN AN AGGREGATE SAMPLE RETURNED (https://www.hou.usra.edu/meetings/lpsc2024/pdf/1640.pdf)\n  47. Enantiomeric excess of amino acids in interstellar ice analogues-asymmetric photolysis of precursors by circularly polarized UV light (https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/517/4/6112/6754748)\n  48. Photonenergy\u2010Controlled Symmetry Breaking with Circularly Polarized Light - Meinert - 2014 - Angewandte Chemie International Edition - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/anie.201307855)\n  49. ENANTIOMERIC EXCESSES INDUCED IN AMINO ACIDS BY ULTRAVIOLET CIRCULARLY POLARIZED LIGHT IRRADIATION OF EXTRATERRESTRIAL ICE ANALOGS: A POSSIBLE SOURCE OF ASYMMETRY FOR PREBIOTIC CHEMISTRY - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/788/1/79)\n  50. Anisotropy-Guided Enantiomeric Enhancement in AlanineUsing Far-UV Circularly Polarized Light (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11084-015-9413-x)\n  51. Homochirality: a prerequisite or consequence of life? (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.12850)\n  52. NASA study reproduces origins of life on ocean floor (https://phys.org/news/2019-02-nasa-life-ocean-floor.html)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.4\n- Feedback: The network is connected, sufficiently populated, and molecule IDs are used consistently, and the high-level literature framing is good: multiple experimentally supported routes converge on rac-alanine, pyruvate is treated as a proximal hub, lactate is tracked as a competing sink, and chirality is separated from racemic synthesis. However, network validity is poor because 9 of 12 reactions are ground-truth UNBALANCED and the checker found 0 additional reactions balanceable by coefficient scaling: rxn_001, rxn_002, rxn_003, rxn_004, rxn_005, rxn_006, rxn_007, rxn_008, and rxn_010. P1 and P2 are not meaningfully distinguished in the current encoding because rxn_001 and rxn_002 are chemically identical apart from labels and omit the distinctive cyanosulfidic reagents. To correct the network, rxn_001 and rxn_002 should be rewritten as Strecker aminonitrile formation plus hydrolysis rather than direct alanine formation; the checker hints missing CO2/O2 reactants. rxn_003 should include hydantoin or N-carbamoyl intermediates and hydrolysis; the checker hints extra CO2 product. rxn_004, rxn_005, rxn_007, and rxn_010 should split alanine and lactate into competing branches rather than fixed coproducts and add missing H2O/CO2 bookkeeping; the checker hints missing CO2 and/or H2O products. rxn_006 and rxn_008 need explicit redox bookkeeping, reduced mineral or electron inputs, and likely H2O or oxime intermediates; the checker hints missing O2 product in the compressed encoding. Only rxn_009, rxn_011, and rxn_012 balance as written, and of those only rxn_009 is a strong synthetic step. Environmental consistency is only moderate because Biochemical is not a real prebiotic environment, key cyanosulfidic and hydrothermal conditions are mostly omitted, and transfer from hydrothermal or aqueous settings to the final surface CPL and wet-dry module is not justified. Literature alignment is conceptually strong, especially the explicit acknowledgement that no known route selectively makes L-alanine, but the final L-alanine module remains speculative. Best current pathway is P9, but the network is not viable in its present form and is not approved.\n- Issues: ['Classical Strecker chemistry is well supported for rac-alanine, but rxn_001 is a ground-truth conservation violation and compresses imine formation, cyanide addition, and nitrile hydrolysis into one step; the checker hints missing CO2/O2 reactants, but the chemically sensible fix is to add an aminopropionitrile intermediate and explicit hydrolysis with water. rxn_011 can at best seed a small enantiomeric excess under specialized CPL irradiation, and rxn_012 is a speculative alanine amplification step rather than a demonstrated route to L-alanine.', 'Cyanosulfidic chemistry is literature-supported, but as encoded P2 is not truly distinct from P1: rxn_002 has the same reactants and products as the classical Strecker node, is unbalanced, and omits the key HS-, Cu(I), UV, and phosphate conditions; the checker again hints missing CO2/O2 reactants. The chiral module remains the main weak point.', 'Bucherer-Bergs from pyruvate is a real prebiotic route, but rxn_003 is unbalanced and overcompressed; the checker hints extra CO2 on the product side. The literature route proceeds through hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates plus hydrolysis, not direct one-step alanine formation. The Biochemical environment label is too vague, and transfer to the surface chiral module is not justified.', 'FeS or Fe(OH)2 reductive amination is plausible under mild alkaline hydrothermal conditions, but rxn_004 is unbalanced; the checker hints missing CO2 product. More importantly, alanine and lactate should be competing branches, not obligatory coproducts of one pyruvate-consuming event. Mineral redox chemistry, high NH3, and transport to the later surface module are not encoded.', 'Green-rust alanine synthesis matches the literature conceptually, but rxn_005 has the same stoichiometric problem as rxn_004 and should not generate alanine and lactate as fixed coproducts; the checker hints missing CO2 product. The decisive mixed-valence Fe(II):Fe(III) condition and alkaline pH are missing, and surface transfer for the chiral module is not justified.', 'Electro-reduced FeS vent chemistry is one of the stronger pyruvate-to-alanine ideas, but rxn_006 still violates conservation; the checker hints missing O2 product, showing that the current one-step encoding lacks explicit water and electron bookkeeping. The need for a sustained negative potential, alkaline NH3-rich conditions, and later transfer to the surface module is not encoded.', 'Ni0 and H2 reductive amination is experimentally supported, but rxn_007 is unbalanced; the checker hints missing H2O/CO2/O2 products. As with the Fe routes, lactate should be a competing side branch rather than a mandatory coproduct, and the hydrothermal setting is underspecified because native Ni, H2 pressure, ammonia activity, and later surface transfer are not described.', 'Oxime reduction from pyruvate is literature-based, but rxn_008 is unbalanced; the checker hints missing O2 product, reflecting the omission of the pyruvate oxime intermediate and Fe0-driven reduction chemistry. Hydroxylamine availability is a major prebiotic weakness, Biochemical is not a useful environment label, and transfer to the surface chiral module is not justified.', 'This is the cleanest encoded precursor route: rxn_009 is balanced and consistent with reported aspartate-to-alanine transamination. However, pyridoxal and metal catalysts are only implicit, the aspartate source sits outside the network, the Biochemical label is vague, and the final claim to L-alanine still rests on weak rxn_011 and highly speculative rxn_012.', 'Weber-type thiol/formose alanine formation is literature-supported at low yield, but rxn_010 is unbalanced; the checker hints missing H2O product. The node omits the required thiol catalyst and likely dehydration intermediates, and again treats lactate as a coproduct rather than a competing sink. Surface conditions are plausible, but the low yield, long timescale, and speculative chiral module limit confidence.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.64\n- Feedback: Connected and mostly internally consistent network: RDKit reports 0 unbalanceable reactions, but rxn_001 should be 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvate + 3 H2O, rxn_002 should be C2H2 + CO + 2 H2O -> pyruvate + H2, and rxn_007 and rxn_009 each need 2 H2O; mol_010 also needs a repairable structure or InChI. Literature alignment is good for the rac-alanine chemistry: Strecker, pyruvate reductive amination, Bucherer-Bergs, and Weber-style aldehyde chemistry are all represented and the network explicitly notes lactate competition, low pyruvate supply, and the chirality bottleneck. The main reason confidence stays moderate is rxn_011; current literature does not support a robust prebiotic full conversion of rac-alanine to L-alanine, only weak or contested small-ee scenarios. Additional issues are the missing CO2 or bicarbonate context for rxn_006, omission of the thiol catalyst in rxn_010, and lack of explicit transport and concentration steps from hydrothermal modules to a surface enrichment setting. Best current pathway is P9, but overall this is a strong modular rac-alanine network rather than a demonstrated L-alanine synthesis network, so it is not approved.\n- Issues: ['Coherent hydrothermal route to rac-alanine, and green-rust amination is experimentally supported. Main weaknesses are the micromolar pyruvate output and missing mineral identity in rxn_001, the use of H2 instead of explicitly redox-active green rust in rxn_003, and the very weakly supported final enantioenrichment.', 'Electroreduced FeS amination is one of the strongest pyruvate-to-alanine modules, but the pathway still inherits the dilute CO2/H2 pyruvate source and does not encode the required negative potential or pre-reduced FeS surface. The final L step remains unsupported.', 'This is the strongest of the CO2/H2-fed hydrothermal variants because Ni/H2 reductive amination is well matched to the written chemistry. Confidence is still limited by low pyruvate supply, the need for Ni and elevated H2 pressure, and the unsupported racemate to L conversion.', 'The pyruvate to hydantoin to alanine bridge is literature-backed, but the implementation misses the experimentally essential CO2 or bicarbonate context for Bucherer-Bergs and rxn_007 needs 2 H2O stoichiometrically. The Biochemical label is not a clear prebiotic environment, and the final L step is weak.', 'Acetylene/CO to pyruvate avoids the very dilute CO2 fixation module, but it needs explicit NiS and temperature control, after which the same green-rust issues from P1 remain. Surface enantioenrichment is again the limiting step.', 'Chemically reasonable modular route: NiS-mediated pyruvate formation followed by electroreduced FeS amination has literature precedents. The missing electrochemical conditions, lack of an explicit transfer to surface, and unsupported L enrichment hold the score down.', 'Probably the strongest hydrothermal chain in the network: both the NiS acetylene/CO pyruvate step and Ni/H2 amination have experimental support. It still needs explicit NiS and 5 bar scale H2 conditions and does not solve the final L-alanine problem.', 'A plausible bridge route on paper, but the Bucherer-Bergs step needs CO2 or bicarbonate in the conditions and the hydrolysis stoichiometry should be corrected. The route also jumps from hot hydrothermal pyruvate generation to a vague Biochemical module and then to surface enantioenrichment without transport justification.', 'Best-aligned pathway. Classical Strecker formation of alpha-aminopropionitrile and subsequent hydrolysis to alanine are robust literature chemistry and fit a surface aqueous setting. The remaining weakness is that rxn_009 should consume 2 H2O and the final step does not yet provide a credible route from rac-alanine to L-alanine.', 'Weber-type aldehyde chemistry is real but low-yield, slow, and thiol-dependent. Because the named thiol catalyst is not represented and the final enantioenrichment is still speculative, this route is weaker than the Strecker module.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-alanine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This evidence-weighted network treats pyruvic acid as the main proximal hub because pyruvate-to-alanine chemistry is the best supported experimental node, while also retaining direct aldehyde/cyanide and HCN-free surface routes on equal footing. Ten pathways are generated by combining two hydrothermal pyruvate-source modules with four pyruvate-to-alanine modules, plus two direct surface routes. All experimentally grounded routes converge on an alanine pool that is expected to be racemic; a final late enantioenrichment node is included explicitly and marked speculative because no robust prebiotic route to selectively make L-alanine is known.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-dihydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyceraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-methylimidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"5-methylhydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(carbamoylamino)propanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"N-carbamoylalanine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alanine (racemic pool)\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(S)-2-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation to pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"greigite (Fe3S4)\", \"magnetite (Fe3O4)\", \"awaruite (Ni3Fe)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"373K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"greigite/magnetite/awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"alkaline H2-rich vent fluid; pyruvate expected only at low concentration\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-assisted reduction of CO2-derived C1 species followed by C-C coupling to a C3 alpha-keto acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pyruvate formation from CO2 and H2 on greigite, magnetite, and awaruite is experimentally supported in alkaline hydrothermal systems, making pyruvic acid a plausible hydrothermal hub even though reported concentrations are only micromolar and concentration remains unresolved (Preiner et al., 2020). This node is therefore included as a low-flux but chemically grounded entry to keto-acid chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"NiS-mediated acetylene/CO carbonylation to pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"nickel sulfide (NiS)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"390-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiS\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"subseafloor carbonylation/hydration chemistry; short residence times favored because pyruvate is thermally labile\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"NiS-promoted acetyleno/carboxydotrophic carbonylation and hydration sequence yielding the C3 alpha-keto acid as a net product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Huber and co-workers reported pyruvate formation from acetylene and CO on NiS above 100 degrees C, providing a distinct hydrothermal C3 entry that does not rely on direct bulk CO2 fixation (Huber et al., 2020). The step is written as a net transformation to keep the network compact; the main critique is that pyruvate is thermally fragile, so residence time at elevated temperature must remain limited (Miller and Bada, 1988).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Green-rust reductive amination of pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"green rust (mixed-valence Fe(II)/Fe(III) oxyhydroxide)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-343K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"green rust\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH about 10; Fe(II):Fe(III) about 1:1; NH3-rich; lower pH favors lactate instead\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvic acid first forms an imine/iminopyruvate with ammonia, then receives a two-electron reduction to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mixed-valence iron oxyhydroxides are among the strongest mild hydrothermal systems for alanine formation from pyruvate, with best alanine selectivity near Fe(II):Fe(III)=1:1 and pH 10, while pH 7 strongly suppresses alanine and favors lactate (Barge et al., 2019). The reaction is written with H2 as the net electron equivalent so the redox balance is explicit, although experimentally the reducing power is carried largely by the mineral redox state itself.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Electroreduced FeS reductive amination of pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"electroreduced FeS/Fe0 surface\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"296-298K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"electroreduced FeS/Fe0\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH 9.6; about 0.5 M NH3; vent-like negative potential required to pre-reduce the mineral\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia condenses with pyruvic acid to an imine equivalent that is reduced on a strongly reducing FeS-derived surface.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Electroreduced FeS generated under vent-like negative potentials aminates pyruvate very efficiently under alkaline conditions, with near-complete conversion reported in favorable runs (Kitadai et al., 2019). Here H2 represents the net reducing equivalent supplied by the pre-reduced FeS/Fe0 surface; the geochemical challenge is sustaining both the reducing potential and NH3-rich conditions in natural systems.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Native nickel/H2 reductive amination of pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"native nickel (Ni0)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni0\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH 7-8; NH3 can be millimolar to tens of millimolar; lower NH3 still gives measurable alanine\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ni0 activates H2 and reduces the imine/iminopyruvate formed from pyruvic acid and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Native nickel under H2 gives one of the most geochemically attractive catalytic pyruvate-to-alanine systems because it works at mild temperature and with substantially less NH3 than older Fe-mineral work, while still giving significant alanine yields (Kaur et al., 2024). Its main weaknesses are the need for available Ni0 and sustained H2 pressure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin formation from pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-323K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"cyanide/ammonia solution; dissolved CO2/HCO3- required mechanistically although not net consumed\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the alpha-keto acid framework, followed by carbamylation/cyclization to the hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pulletikurti and co-workers showed that alpha-keto acids enter Bucherer-Bergs chemistry under prebiotically relevant aqueous conditions, and that dissolved CO2/HCO3- is mechanistically essential even when it is not net consumed (Pulletikurti et al., 2022). This is an important bridge between metabolic carbon skeletons and cyanide chemistry, but it inherits the unresolved requirement that pyruvate and cyanide must be co-localized.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic ring opening of 5-methylhydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-333K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"mild hydrolytic ring opening in aqueous solution\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water opens the hydantoin ring to the corresponding N-carbamoyl amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ring opening of hydantoins to N-carbamoyl amino acids is the expected next stage of Bucherer-Bergs chemistry and is chemically more realistic than collapsing the whole route into a single pyruvate-to-alanine step (Pulletikurti et al., 2022). Including this intermediate preserves mechanistic continuity and stoichiometric correctness.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis and decarbamylation of N-carbamoylalanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-333K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"continued aqueous processing to release alanine\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Further hydrolysis cleaves the carbamoyl group, releasing alanine along with CO2 and NH3.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Once the N-carbamoyl intermediate is formed, its hydrolysis closes the Bucherer-Bergs branch cleanly to alanine without requiring an unsupported catalyst (Pulletikurti et al., 2022). This step also makes explicit the release of CO2 and NH3 that is hidden in overcompressed one-step encodings.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker aminonitrile formation from acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"HCN-rich aqueous pool; adequate NH3 suppresses diversion toward cyanohydrin chemistry\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetaldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, and cyanide capture gives the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the classical Strecker entry to alanine. Miller-type discharge chemistry and later cyanosulfidic networks both supply compatible HCN and aldehyde feedstocks, while isotopic work supports Strecker chemistry as a real source of alanine in natural samples (Miller, 1953; Cleaves et al., 2008; Patel et al., 2015; Chimiak et al., 2022).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of alpha-aminopropionitrile to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"prolonged hydration during acidic/basic cycling or wet-dry reworking\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The nitrile is hydrated through amide-like intermediates to the carboxylic acid, releasing ammonia overall.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrolysis of the aminonitrile is the second canonical Strecker stage and should be represented explicitly because water is chemically required for conversion to alanine (Miller, 1953; Chimiak et al., 2022). This separation avoids hiding nitrile hydrolysis inside an overpacked one-step Strecker node.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiol-catalyzed triose route from glyceraldehyde to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"3-mercaptopropionic acid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"313K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"3-mercaptopropionic acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH 5.2; 35-60 day timescale; low yield\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thiol-assisted dehydration of glyceraldehyde to methylglyoxal-like intermediates is followed by imine/hemithioacetal chemistry and hydrolysis to alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Weber demonstrated that glyceraldehyde plus ammonia can yield alanine in water and that a thiol catalyst accelerates the chemistry markedly, making this a distinct HCN-free surface route (Weber, 1985; Weber, 1998). The drawbacks are low yield, long timescale, and the unresolved prebiotic source of the thiol catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Speculative enantioenrichment of alanine pool to L-alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"200-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"false\",\n        \"other\": \"dry-film or ice-residue UV irradiation followed by wet-dry recrystallization; only small ee is demonstrated experimentally\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Weak enantioselective photolysis or differential survival seeds a small L excess that is then assumed to undergo physical amplification during repeated cycling.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No experimentally supported prebiotic route makes L-alanine directly. CPL photolysis can induce only percent-level enantiomeric excesses in alanine or its precursors, and natural claims of substantial extraterrestrial L-alanine excess are disputed; later Murchison and Bennu measurements are racemic (Meinert et al., 2014; Modica et al., 2014; Pizzarello and Cronin, 1998; Glavin et al., 2021; Glavin, Dworkin et al., 2025). This node is therefore explicitly speculative and is included only because the requested target is L-alanine rather than racemic alanine.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation plus green-rust amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation supplies pyruvic acid, which is aminated on green rust before a late speculative surface enrichment step.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation plus electroreduced FeS amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation supplies pyruvic acid, which is aminated on electroreduced FeS/Fe0 and then passed to the late enrichment module.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation plus native Ni/H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation supplies pyruvic acid, which is aminated on native nickel under H2 before speculative L-selection.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2 fixation plus Bucherer-Bergs bridge\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal pyruvic acid is handed off to cyanide-rich surface chemistry, where Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin chemistry yields alanine before late enrichment; co-localization of pyruvate and cyanide is the open problem.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene/CO route plus green-rust amination\",\n      \"description\": \"NiS-mediated hydrothermal carbonylation of acetylene and CO forms pyruvic acid, followed by green-rust amination and late enrichment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene/CO route plus electroreduced FeS amination\",\n      \"description\": \"NiS-mediated hydrothermal pyruvic acid formation is coupled to electroreduced FeS amination, then to the speculative L-selection module.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene/CO route plus native Ni/H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"A hydrothermal acetyleno/carboxydotrophic pyruvic acid source is combined with native Ni/H2 amination before late enrichment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene/CO route plus Bucherer-Bergs bridge\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal pyruvic acid from acetylene/CO chemistry is bridged into cyanide-bearing surface pools through Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin chemistry, then sent to late enrichment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct surface Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde, HCN, and ammonia form alpha-aminopropionitrile, which hydrolyzes to alanine before the late speculative enrichment step.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct thiol-assisted triose route\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyceraldehyde and ammonia react in a thiol-catalyzed surface route to alanine, followed by the explicit but weakly supported L-enrichment module.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_014\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_014\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "L-alanine", "overall_strategy": "This evidence-weighted network treats pyruvic acid as the main proximal hub because pyruvate-to-alanine chemistry is the best supported experimental node, while also retaining direct aldehyde/cyanide and HCN-free surface routes on equal footing. Ten pathways are generated by combining two hydrothermal pyruvate-source modules with four pyruvate-to-alanine modules, plus two direct surface routes. All experimentally grounded routes converge on an alanine pool that is expected to be racemic; a final late enantioenrichment node is included explicitly and marked speculative because no robust prebiotic route to selectively make L-alanine is known.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "ethyne", "common_name": "acetylene", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CO", "iupac_name": "carbon monoxide", "common_name": "carbon monoxide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2,3-dihydroxypropanal", "common_name": "glyceraldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "alpha-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H6N2O2", "iupac_name": "5-methylimidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "5-methylhydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H8N2O3", "iupac_name": "2-(carbamoylamino)propanoic acid", "common_name": "N-carbamoylalanine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "alanine (racemic pool)", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C3H7NO2", "iupac_name": "(S)-2-aminopropanoic acid", "common_name": "L-alanine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)/t2-/m0/s1", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation to pyruvic acid", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["greigite (Fe3S4)", "magnetite (Fe3O4)", "awaruite (Ni3Fe)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "373K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "greigite/magnetite/awaruite", "aqueous": "true", "other": "alkaline H2-rich vent fluid; pyruvate expected only at low concentration"}, "mechanism": "Mineral-assisted reduction of CO2-derived C1 species followed by C-C coupling to a C3 alpha-keto acid.", "reasoning": "Pyruvate formation from CO2 and H2 on greigite, magnetite, and awaruite is experimentally supported in alkaline hydrothermal systems, making pyruvic acid a plausible hydrothermal hub even though reported concentrations are only micromolar and concentration remains unresolved (Preiner et al., 2020). This node is therefore included as a low-flux but chemically grounded entry to keto-acid chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "NiS-mediated acetylene/CO carbonylation to pyruvic acid", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["nickel sulfide (NiS)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "390-430K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "NiS", "aqueous": "true", "other": "subseafloor carbonylation/hydration chemistry; short residence times favored because pyruvate is thermally labile"}, "mechanism": "NiS-promoted acetyleno/carboxydotrophic carbonylation and hydration sequence yielding the C3 alpha-keto acid as a net product.", "reasoning": "Huber and co-workers reported pyruvate formation from acetylene and CO on NiS above 100 degrees C, providing a distinct hydrothermal C3 entry that does not rely on direct bulk CO2 fixation (Huber et al., 2020). The step is written as a net transformation to keep the network compact; the main critique is that pyruvate is thermally fragile, so residence time at elevated temperature must remain limited (Miller and Bada, 1988)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Green-rust reductive amination of pyruvic acid", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["green rust (mixed-valence Fe(II)/Fe(III) oxyhydroxide)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-343K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "green rust", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH about 10; Fe(II):Fe(III) about 1:1; NH3-rich; lower pH favors lactate instead"}, "mechanism": "Pyruvic acid first forms an imine/iminopyruvate with ammonia, then receives a two-electron reduction to the amino acid.", "reasoning": "Mixed-valence iron oxyhydroxides are among the strongest mild hydrothermal systems for alanine formation from pyruvate, with best alanine selectivity near Fe(II):Fe(III)=1:1 and pH 10, while pH 7 strongly suppresses alanine and favors lactate (Barge et al., 2019). The reaction is written with H2 as the net electron equivalent so the redox balance is explicit, although experimentally the reducing power is carried largely by the mineral redox state itself."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Electroreduced FeS reductive amination of pyruvic acid", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["electroreduced FeS/Fe0 surface"], "conditions": {"temperature": "296-298K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "electroreduced FeS/Fe0", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH 9.6; about 0.5 M NH3; vent-like negative potential required to pre-reduce the mineral"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia condenses with pyruvic acid to an imine equivalent that is reduced on a strongly reducing FeS-derived surface.", "reasoning": "Electroreduced FeS generated under vent-like negative potentials aminates pyruvate very efficiently under alkaline conditions, with near-complete conversion reported in favorable runs (Kitadai et al., 2019). Here H2 represents the net reducing equivalent supplied by the pre-reduced FeS/Fe0 surface; the geochemical challenge is sustaining both the reducing potential and NH3-rich conditions in natural systems."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Native nickel/H2 reductive amination of pyruvic acid", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["native nickel (Ni0)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-300K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni0", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH 7-8; NH3 can be millimolar to tens of millimolar; lower NH3 still gives measurable alanine"}, "mechanism": "Ni0 activates H2 and reduces the imine/iminopyruvate formed from pyruvic acid and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Native nickel under H2 gives one of the most geochemically attractive catalytic pyruvate-to-alanine systems because it works at mild temperature and with substantially less NH3 than older Fe-mineral work, while still giving significant alanine yields (Kaur et al., 2024). Its main weaknesses are the need for available Ni0 and sustained H2 pressure."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin formation from pyruvic acid", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "298-323K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "cyanide/ammonia solution; dissolved CO2/HCO3- required mechanistically although not net consumed"}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the alpha-keto acid framework, followed by carbamylation/cyclization to the hydantoin.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti and co-workers showed that alpha-keto acids enter Bucherer-Bergs chemistry under prebiotically relevant aqueous conditions, and that dissolved CO2/HCO3- is mechanistically essential even when it is not net consumed (Pulletikurti et al., 2022). This is an important bridge between metabolic carbon skeletons and cyanide chemistry, but it inherits the unresolved requirement that pyruvate and cyanide must be co-localized."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrolytic ring opening of 5-methylhydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "298-333K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "mild hydrolytic ring opening in aqueous solution"}, "mechanism": "Water opens the hydantoin ring to the corresponding N-carbamoyl amino acid.", "reasoning": "Ring opening of hydantoins to N-carbamoyl amino acids is the expected next stage of Bucherer-Bergs chemistry and is chemically more realistic than collapsing the whole route into a single pyruvate-to-alanine step (Pulletikurti et al., 2022). Including this intermediate preserves mechanistic continuity and stoichiometric correctness."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrolysis and decarbamylation of N-carbamoylalanine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "298-333K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "continued aqueous processing to release alanine"}, "mechanism": "Further hydrolysis cleaves the carbamoyl group, releasing alanine along with CO2 and NH3.", "reasoning": "Once the N-carbamoyl intermediate is formed, its hydrolysis closes the Bucherer-Bergs branch cleanly to alanine without requiring an unsupported catalyst (Pulletikurti et al., 2022). This step also makes explicit the release of CO2 and NH3 that is hidden in overcompressed one-step encodings."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Strecker aminonitrile formation from acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "HCN-rich aqueous pool; adequate NH3 suppresses diversion toward cyanohydrin chemistry"}, "mechanism": "Acetaldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, and cyanide capture gives the alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the classical Strecker entry to alanine. Miller-type discharge chemistry and later cyanosulfidic networks both supply compatible HCN and aldehyde feedstocks, while isotopic work supports Strecker chemistry as a real source of alanine in natural samples (Miller, 1953; Cleaves et al., 2008; Patel et al., 2015; Chimiak et al., 2022)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydrolysis of alpha-aminopropionitrile to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "prolonged hydration during acidic/basic cycling or wet-dry reworking"}, "mechanism": "The nitrile is hydrated through amide-like intermediates to the carboxylic acid, releasing ammonia overall.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysis of the aminonitrile is the second canonical Strecker stage and should be represented explicitly because water is chemically required for conversion to alanine (Miller, 1953; Chimiak et al., 2022). This separation avoids hiding nitrile hydrolysis inside an overpacked one-step Strecker node."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Thiol-catalyzed triose route from glyceraldehyde to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["3-mercaptopropionic acid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "313K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "3-mercaptopropionic acid", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH 5.2; 35-60 day timescale; low yield"}, "mechanism": "Thiol-assisted dehydration of glyceraldehyde to methylglyoxal-like intermediates is followed by imine/hemithioacetal chemistry and hydrolysis to alanine.", "reasoning": "Weber demonstrated that glyceraldehyde plus ammonia can yield alanine in water and that a thiol catalyst accelerates the chemistry markedly, making this a distinct HCN-free surface route (Weber, 1985; Weber, 1998). The drawbacks are low yield, long timescale, and the unresolved prebiotic source of the thiol catalyst."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Speculative enantioenrichment of alanine pool to L-alanine", "inputs": ["mol_014"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "200-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "false", "other": "dry-film or ice-residue UV irradiation followed by wet-dry recrystallization; only small ee is demonstrated experimentally"}, "mechanism": "Weak enantioselective photolysis or differential survival seeds a small L excess that is then assumed to undergo physical amplification during repeated cycling.", "reasoning": "No experimentally supported prebiotic route makes L-alanine directly. CPL photolysis can induce only percent-level enantiomeric excesses in alanine or its precursors, and natural claims of substantial extraterrestrial L-alanine excess are disputed; later Murchison and Bennu measurements are racemic (Meinert et al., 2014; Modica et al., 2014; Pizzarello and Cronin, 1998; Glavin et al., 2021; Glavin, Dworkin et al., 2025). This node is therefore explicitly speculative and is included only because the requested target is L-alanine rather than racemic alanine."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "CO2 fixation plus green-rust amination", "description": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation supplies pyruvic acid, which is aminated on green rust before a late speculative surface enrichment step.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "CO2 fixation plus electroreduced FeS amination", "description": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation supplies pyruvic acid, which is aminated on electroreduced FeS/Fe0 and then passed to the late enrichment module.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_004", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "CO2 fixation plus native Ni/H2 amination", "description": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation supplies pyruvic acid, which is aminated on native nickel under H2 before speculative L-selection.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_005", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "CO2 fixation plus Bucherer-Bergs bridge", "description": "Hydrothermal pyruvic acid is handed off to cyanide-rich surface chemistry, where Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin chemistry yields alanine before late enrichment; co-localization of pyruvate and cyanide is the open problem.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010", "mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Acetylene/CO route plus green-rust amination", "description": "NiS-mediated hydrothermal carbonylation of acetylene and CO forms pyruvic acid, followed by green-rust amination and late e